{:db/id "answer-field-c9b3ff18-ad06-567e-8eeb-6c0aba04f8f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9b3ff18-ad06-567e-8eeb-6c0aba04f8f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fa5f2cfc-cb8c-52c8-819f-3bad956779a7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-883faa6f-39e1-5ebb-8a95-1d13f2f0cba0" "answer-14c882a4-ff2e-52f0-b361-1b4fc56ae096" "answer-fa5f2cfc-cb8c-52c8-819f-3bad956779a7" "answer-2fa004c3-5d69-511c-9a6f-4fd59bec35fd" "answer-e5994c61-3dc1-59f0-899f-597ae5e4f768"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-883faa6f-39e1-5ebb-8a95-1d13f2f0cba0" :answer/id #uuid "883faa6f-39e1-5ebb-8a95-1d13f2f0cba0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14c882a4-ff2e-52f0-b361-1b4fc56ae096" :answer/id #uuid "14c882a4-ff2e-52f0-b361-1b4fc56ae096" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa5f2cfc-cb8c-52c8-819f-3bad956779a7" :answer/id #uuid "fa5f2cfc-cb8c-52c8-819f-3bad956779a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{40}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fa004c3-5d69-511c-9a6f-4fd59bec35fd" :answer/id #uuid "2fa004c3-5d69-511c-9a6f-4fd59bec35fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5994c61-3dc1-59f0-899f-597ae5e4f768" :answer/id #uuid "e5994c61-3dc1-59f0-899f-597ae5e4f768" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-97be54c9-28e3-5dbb-97cf-64bffd902d5f" :question/id #uuid "97be54c9-28e3-5dbb-97cf-64bffd902d5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-4304" :question/problem "![](images/60/606a6eb139d8e5813f85c608824d8313a97ddaa465b7c05c89173247550a77ee.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $ABCD$ shown above, determine the measure of $∠C.$" :question/worked-solution "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral  must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (x+{30}^{∘})+{75}^{∘}+5x+(3x-{15}^{∘}) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 9x+{90}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 9x & ={270}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & =5x \\\\  & =5({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={150}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-21cbe6a6-724d-5863-82b3-b2dda90eaa9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-21cbe6a6-724d-5863-82b3-b2dda90eaa9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "21cbe6a6-724d-5863-82b3-b2dda90eaa9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-50a09afb-1a3c-51a0-85da-c404d79a9d7e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cddf5b4d-4b52-50b1-a7aa-a6060fdc403f" "answer-78dc7f20-458e-59be-adc9-3e8c6383f239" "answer-50a09afb-1a3c-51a0-85da-c404d79a9d7e" "answer-a1f780f9-2d19-552a-81f5-1f0f523ddd83" "answer-53e7832a-ea74-5c30-8ca2-fc9454520d6f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cddf5b4d-4b52-50b1-a7aa-a6060fdc403f" :answer/id #uuid "cddf5b4d-4b52-50b1-a7aa-a6060fdc403f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{130}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78dc7f20-458e-59be-adc9-3e8c6383f239" :answer/id #uuid "78dc7f20-458e-59be-adc9-3e8c6383f239" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{135}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-50a09afb-1a3c-51a0-85da-c404d79a9d7e" :answer/id #uuid "50a09afb-1a3c-51a0-85da-c404d79a9d7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{150}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1f780f9-2d19-552a-81f5-1f0f523ddd83" :answer/id #uuid "a1f780f9-2d19-552a-81f5-1f0f523ddd83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{170}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53e7832a-ea74-5c30-8ca2-fc9454520d6f" :answer/id #uuid "53e7832a-ea74-5c30-8ca2-fc9454520d6f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{205}^{∘}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-be9eb345-ad38-54e4-ae93-ee7a03d3fd33" :question/id #uuid "be9eb345-ad38-54e4-ae93-ee7a03d3fd33" :question/math-academy-id "q-74243" :question/problem "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is ${360}^{∘}.$ How many sides does it have?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is ${180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)={360}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{{360}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =2 \\\\ n & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=4$ sides (so it is a quadrilateral)." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-38e8cc93-af12-5bc4-8221-a544d7de6f94"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-38e8cc93-af12-5bc4-8221-a544d7de6f94" :answer-field/id #uuid "38e8cc93-af12-5bc4-8221-a544d7de6f94" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-14fab282-cea6-54d6-9c7b-6abd288a09e4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ddded419-4708-5c4c-98ca-3fea86e4d404" "answer-14fab282-cea6-54d6-9c7b-6abd288a09e4" "answer-ac0ddcb8-b0bf-516b-9997-05324f124420" "answer-cddfe483-9794-51a8-83e5-7afd6219f922" "answer-a1dd7d91-8551-513b-88c0-f9d5414ec035"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ddded419-4708-5c4c-98ca-3fea86e4d404" :answer/id #uuid "ddded419-4708-5c4c-98ca-3fea86e4d404" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14fab282-cea6-54d6-9c7b-6abd288a09e4" :answer/id #uuid "14fab282-cea6-54d6-9c7b-6abd288a09e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac0ddcb8-b0bf-516b-9997-05324f124420" :answer/id #uuid "ac0ddcb8-b0bf-516b-9997-05324f124420" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cddfe483-9794-51a8-83e5-7afd6219f922" :answer/id #uuid "cddfe483-9794-51a8-83e5-7afd6219f922" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1dd7d91-8551-513b-88c0-f9d5414ec035" :answer/id #uuid "a1dd7d91-8551-513b-88c0-f9d5414ec035" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4852498b-dc1b-525e-82e2-305f7ebaf1ab" :question/id #uuid "4852498b-dc1b-525e-82e2-305f7ebaf1ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-23002" :question/problem "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is ${2700}^{∘}.$ How many sides does it have?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is ${180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)=2\\,{700}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & =2\\,{700}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{2\\,{700}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =15 \\\\ n & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=17$ sides." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6767309d-2860-5c8c-9e6e-30cf9071e907"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6767309d-2860-5c8c-9e6e-30cf9071e907" :answer-field/id #uuid "6767309d-2860-5c8c-9e6e-30cf9071e907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cbe7ea46-ddc5-572a-81b6-c5feb2fff757" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1ab977e1-87f4-5761-86af-f2fe440d0bfd" "answer-cbe7ea46-ddc5-572a-81b6-c5feb2fff757" "answer-8696ad5f-9f95-570c-9894-0b78bfff2ece" "answer-542b3dea-7943-560d-ad8d-d052f329538e" "answer-47613396-5fe7-586c-bc66-d0105e8e4f49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ab977e1-87f4-5761-86af-f2fe440d0bfd" :answer/id #uuid "1ab977e1-87f4-5761-86af-f2fe440d0bfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbe7ea46-ddc5-572a-81b6-c5feb2fff757" :answer/id #uuid "cbe7ea46-ddc5-572a-81b6-c5feb2fff757" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8696ad5f-9f95-570c-9894-0b78bfff2ece" :answer/id #uuid "8696ad5f-9f95-570c-9894-0b78bfff2ece" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-542b3dea-7943-560d-ad8d-d052f329538e" :answer/id #uuid "542b3dea-7943-560d-ad8d-d052f329538e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47613396-5fe7-586c-bc66-d0105e8e4f49" :answer/id #uuid "47613396-5fe7-586c-bc66-d0105e8e4f49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3c97d79e-a33f-5989-8625-5100ed2853d5" :question/id #uuid "3c97d79e-a33f-5989-8625-5100ed2853d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-118066" :question/problem "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon is ${3240}^{∘}.$ How many sides does it have?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides is ${180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).$\n\nTo find the number of sides in our polygon, we start by setting up the following equation:\n\n$$\n{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2)=3\\,{240}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & =3\\,{240}^{∘} \\\\ n-2 & =\\frac{3\\,{240}^{∘}}{{180}^{∘}} \\\\ n-2 & =18 \\\\ n & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polygon has $n=20$ sides." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a5105cc6-559d-580a-970e-90938d53d8c0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a5105cc6-559d-580a-970e-90938d53d8c0" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5105cc6-559d-580a-970e-90938d53d8c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c3532b78-c577-5fb9-8d13-a69f3b9f6069" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f860660-0b31-504d-911d-5f4129c53d29" "answer-c3532b78-c577-5fb9-8d13-a69f3b9f6069" "answer-52b718f0-59f4-52f4-9ebf-fa4f00a5fd44" "answer-e0c0acdb-523a-5bb3-b8f3-8cf06d38ca56" "answer-d79da2d8-96d6-5f93-8a67-85c203683b8a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f860660-0b31-504d-911d-5f4129c53d29" :answer/id #uuid "3f860660-0b31-504d-911d-5f4129c53d29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "19" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3532b78-c577-5fb9-8d13-a69f3b9f6069" :answer/id #uuid "c3532b78-c577-5fb9-8d13-a69f3b9f6069" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52b718f0-59f4-52f4-9ebf-fa4f00a5fd44" :answer/id #uuid "52b718f0-59f4-52f4-9ebf-fa4f00a5fd44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0c0acdb-523a-5bb3-b8f3-8cf06d38ca56" :answer/id #uuid "e0c0acdb-523a-5bb3-b8f3-8cf06d38ca56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "26" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d79da2d8-96d6-5f93-8a67-85c203683b8a" :answer/id #uuid "d79da2d8-96d6-5f93-8a67-85c203683b8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107137 :tutorial/content "The sum of the interior angles of *any* triangle equals $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/fc/fcc21c5e13ab095da35eeca128e3a82a075d649e205aa0e1683b6ab0e4a4cc4c.png)\n\nAs it turns out, there is a general rule for the sum of the interior angles that applies to all polygons:\n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nFor example, every triangle has ${\\color{blue}3}$ sides. Let's substitute $n={\\color{blue}3}$ into the formula to check that it works:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (3-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a triangle equals $180^\\circ,$ which we know is correct.\n\nLet's see some more examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107138 :tutorial/content "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery quadrilateral has $n={\\color{blue}4}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral equals $360^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/a0/a0d6cd7aa4b4e7aba6451b32087fa6cf86e0f63d2ff70e68328a5ac9d4eddcfc.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107139 :tutorial/content "The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals \n\n$$\n180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).\n$$\n\nEvery pentagon has $n={\\color{blue}5}$ sides. Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ={540}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula tells us that the sum of the interior angles of a pentagon equals $540^\\circ.$ \n\n![](images/8d/8d0478edf5e40b947b06b7b6b0b726999b11ce39dc8af5daaf414f2bd2292647.png)\n\nWe can use the same formula to deduce the sum of the interior angles of hexagons, heptagons, octagons, and so forth." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107170 :tutorial/content "We've made use of the following theorem throughout this lesson. \n\n*The sum of the interior angles of a polygon with $n$ sides equals $180^\\circ \\cdot (n-2).$*\n\nThis formula works because any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles using diagonals that meet at one fixed vertex of the polygon. \n\nLet's illustrate this for a few different polygons.\n\n| ![](images/20/20aae267ba75bb418e509d548b1519af47b4cf9464d7f8400369c8e77b013aae.png) | **Quadrilateral** \n\nNumber of vertices: $4$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $4 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{2}} = 360^\\circ$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| ![](images/20/20551ad9f4661402aef9e501bf9d4c841e671b4277fae78f85d9e9ada9830592.png) | **Pentagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $5$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $5 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{3}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}} = 540^\\circ$ |\n| ![](images/de/de8ec9be742a40384891de3a682a151e817f6c24d624e0ef505fb98ce6a1e60d.png) | **Hexagon** \n\nNumber of vertices: $6$ \n\nNumber of triangles: $6 - 2 = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ \n\nSum of interior angles: $180^\\circ \\cdot {\\color{blue}{4}} = 720^\\circ$ |\n\nThe sum of the interior angles of each polygon is the same as the sum of the interior angles of the corresponding triangles. \n\nEach triangle has interior angles that sum to $180^\\circ,$ and any $n$-sided polygon can be split into $n-2$ triangles in the manner shown above. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles in an $n$-sided polygon is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105973 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125063 :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}-{x}^{2}-2{e}^{y}$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186351509 :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(0,0)\\in D.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186354504 :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2x-1$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(1,0)\\in D.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284576 :question/problem "Find the global maximum of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+2y-1$ on the closed square $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.$ You're given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,1),$ and on the boundary of the square the points that could give rise to extreme values are the vertices." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186363541 :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2y$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 3\\}.$ You are given that the function has a critical point at $(0,-1)\\in D.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-1da851cc-95e3-5599-ae77-2e4ff094e963" :activity/id #uuid "1da851cc-95e3-5599-ae77-2e4ff094e963" :activity/title "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048450 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-7e5430d8-004e-5647-bf66-5de0abfbdad8" "step-33ee7691-7413-57d9-a695-13ab29e0b850" "step-00ed6b29-03a3-54e9-aaeb-46a22b4189a3" "step-7664bde3-a5cc-5860-8f11-c8f5b04ca956" "step-2e2150ac-f1ac-560d-bcf7-a241180b1570"] :activity/first-step "step-7e5430d8-004e-5647-bf66-5de0abfbdad8" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-7e5430d8-004e-5647-bf66-5de0abfbdad8" :step/id #uuid "7e5430d8-004e-5647-bf66-5de0abfbdad8" :step/content 17592186125063 :step/next "step-33ee7691-7413-57d9-a695-13ab29e0b850" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-33ee7691-7413-57d9-a695-13ab29e0b850" :step/id #uuid "33ee7691-7413-57d9-a695-13ab29e0b850" :step/content 17592186351509 :step/next "step-00ed6b29-03a3-54e9-aaeb-46a22b4189a3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-00ed6b29-03a3-54e9-aaeb-46a22b4189a3" :step/id #uuid "00ed6b29-03a3-54e9-aaeb-46a22b4189a3" :step/content 17592186354504 :step/next "step-7664bde3-a5cc-5860-8f11-c8f5b04ca956" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7664bde3-a5cc-5860-8f11-c8f5b04ca956" :step/id #uuid "7664bde3-a5cc-5860-8f11-c8f5b04ca956" :step/content 17592186284576 :step/next "step-2e2150ac-f1ac-560d-bcf7-a241180b1570" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2e2150ac-f1ac-560d-bcf7-a241180b1570" :step/id #uuid "2e2150ac-f1ac-560d-bcf7-a241180b1570" :step/content 17592186363541 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186268835 :answer-field/choices 17592186266818] [:db/retract 17592186268835 :answer-field/choices 17592186268226] [:db/retract 17592186268835 :answer-field/choices 17592186268357] [:db/retract 17592186268835 :answer-field/choices 17592186267080] [:db/retract 17592186268835 :answer-field/choices 17592186266968] [:db/retract 17592186268663 :answer-field/choices 17592186266462] [:db/retract 17592186268663 :answer-field/choices 17592186267221] [:db/retract 17592186268663 :answer-field/choices 17592186268070] [:db/retract 17592186268663 :answer-field/choices 17592186266600] [:db/retract 17592186268663 :answer-field/choices 17592186266190] {:db/id 17592186335769 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(1,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,1) & =2(1+1)-2=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(1,1) & ={f}_{x}(1,1)(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ z-1 & =2x-2 \\\\ z & =2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(1,1,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=2x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=2x-1$ (or $2x-z-1=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨2,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $-i-2\\,k$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-(-i-2\\,k)=i+2\\,k$ is also parallel to it." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124438 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac629eca-77ed-5bb7-99e7-49845bdc5d9a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac629eca-77ed-5bb7-99e7-49845bdc5d9a" :question/id #uuid "ac629eca-77ed-5bb7-99e7-49845bdc5d9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-71204" :question/problem "The curve $C$ is the intersection of a surface $S,$ defined by $z=f(x,y),$ with the plane $y=0.$ Given that the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ lies on $C$, and that ${f}_{x}=(y+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi x)+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)$, find the equations of the tangent line to $C$ at $P.$" :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $y=0.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂x}$ at $(\\frac{1}{2},0)\\,:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =(0+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂x}{|}_{(\\frac{1}{2},0)}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =1(x-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ z-1 & =x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z & =x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\frac{1}{2},0,1)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1450b1c6-e44c-5c3f-b403-2b991fbf5f5c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1450b1c6-e44c-5c3f-b403-2b991fbf5f5c" :answer-field/id #uuid "1450b1c6-e44c-5c3f-b403-2b991fbf5f5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4f28db71-8536-543c-a65e-9e2f2a45d22b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f1fec6bc-c021-533e-ab85-5127fbbe71f4" "answer-4ecbc247-a7b4-51ad-ac21-7f50b73d7f02" "answer-022e71c7-1d96-5b12-ab9b-3c65246aca6e" "answer-cbf544a9-31d0-5386-9054-024015e9e1e0" "answer-4f28db71-8536-543c-a65e-9e2f2a45d22b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1fec6bc-c021-533e-ab85-5127fbbe71f4" :answer/id #uuid "f1fec6bc-c021-533e-ab85-5127fbbe71f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=2x+1\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ecbc247-a7b4-51ad-ac21-7f50b73d7f02" :answer/id #uuid "4ecbc247-a7b4-51ad-ac21-7f50b73d7f02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=2x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-022e71c7-1d96-5b12-ab9b-3c65246aca6e" :answer/id #uuid "022e71c7-1d96-5b12-ab9b-3c65246aca6e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=2x\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbf544a9-31d0-5386-9054-024015e9e1e0" :answer/id #uuid "cbf544a9-31d0-5386-9054-024015e9e1e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=3x-2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4f28db71-8536-543c-a65e-9e2f2a45d22b" :answer/id #uuid "4f28db71-8536-543c-a65e-9e2f2a45d22b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}y=0 \\\\ z=x+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268887 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=2.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi /4)}(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z-0 & =4(y-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ z & =4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(2,\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268835 :answer-field/correct "answer-234ff552-30db-5efc-99f4-6ca2b3ba1ac1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8405b123-1df2-58c7-a42b-133a4390e156" "answer-6b8f64a9-ff65-58b8-9065-4abeb8498b0d" "answer-89e9a579-0e60-5841-a0a0-a7fc520c336b" "answer-234ff552-30db-5efc-99f4-6ca2b3ba1ac1" "answer-9b5f83b9-acd0-55f1-b0be-802d7971c9b8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8405b123-1df2-58c7-a42b-133a4390e156" :answer/id #uuid "8405b123-1df2-58c7-a42b-133a4390e156" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=2y+\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b8f64a9-ff65-58b8-9065-4abeb8498b0d" :answer/id #uuid "6b8f64a9-ff65-58b8-9065-4abeb8498b0d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=2y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89e9a579-0e60-5841-a0a0-a7fc520c336b" :answer/id #uuid "89e9a579-0e60-5841-a0a0-a7fc520c336b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-234ff552-30db-5efc-99f4-6ca2b3ba1ac1" :answer/id #uuid "234ff552-30db-5efc-99f4-6ca2b3ba1ac1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y-\\pi \\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b5f83b9-acd0-55f1-b0be-802d7971c9b8" :answer/id #uuid "9b5f83b9-acd0-55f1-b0be-802d7971c9b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=2 \\\\ z=4y\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268888 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface $z=f(x,y)$ with the plane $x=\\sqrt{2}.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ will be given by the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}.$\n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $\\frac{∂z}{∂y}$ at $(\\sqrt{2},2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(\\sqrt{2},2) & =-\\frac{2(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+2}{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4+2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-z{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)} & =\\frac{∂z}{∂y}{|}_{(\\sqrt{2},2)}(y-2) \\\\ z-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{3}{4}(y-2) \\\\ z & =-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(\\sqrt{2},2,\\frac{1}{2})$ can be given as the intersection of two planes, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268663 :answer-field/correct "answer-7344417b-833c-54ed-9d9c-2ee1f6469356" :answer-field/choices ["answer-821cfb95-3293-5dd2-ae19-a95141c69336" "answer-c6fb22f8-31af-560a-a251-6124b9e4d0a4" "answer-7344417b-833c-54ed-9d9c-2ee1f6469356" "answer-a05b6b84-89fc-51b8-b22e-5a5a84b42efd" "answer-1d6e4604-362d-57a9-a29b-e6f955c20002"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-821cfb95-3293-5dd2-ae19-a95141c69336" :answer/id #uuid "821cfb95-3293-5dd2-ae19-a95141c69336" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{1}{4}y-2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6fb22f8-31af-560a-a251-6124b9e4d0a4" :answer/id #uuid "c6fb22f8-31af-560a-a251-6124b9e4d0a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{2}y+1\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7344417b-833c-54ed-9d9c-2ee1f6469356" :answer/id #uuid "7344417b-833c-54ed-9d9c-2ee1f6469356" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=-\\frac{3}{4}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a05b6b84-89fc-51b8-b22e-5a5a84b42efd" :answer/id #uuid "a05b6b84-89fc-51b8-b22e-5a5a84b42efd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{1}{2}y+2\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d6e4604-362d-57a9-a29b-e6f955c20002" :answer/id #uuid "1d6e4604-362d-57a9-a29b-e6f955c20002" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{\\begin{aligned}x=\\sqrt{2} \\\\ z=\\frac{3}{2}y-1\\end{aligned}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186268893 :question/worked-solution "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ Hence, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ at $(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+{1}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0+1) \\\\  & =0+1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-7 & =1(x) \\\\ z & =x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,7)$ can be given as the intersection of two planes:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1 \\\\ z=x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $y=1,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨0,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=x+7$ (or $x-z+7=0$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,-1⟩.$\n\nFinally, to find a direction vector for the tangent line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨0,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $⟨-1,0,-1⟩$ is parallel to the tangent line, then $-⟨-1,0,-1⟩=⟨1,0,1⟩$ is also parallel to it." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-750348b2-caa7-58e2-bdab-6df9b8b73e30" :activity/id #uuid "750348b2-caa7-58e2-bdab-6df9b8b73e30" :activity/title "Geometric Interpretations of Partial Derivatives" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050158 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-f19a2237-84fe-5d2b-8a00-d94a6ea46590" "step-c446fed3-ebea-5ea0-9317-067e792c87d5" "step-a13b050c-9930-5688-bdc6-1a08f5d3cefe" "step-97bb20f0-d68d-5f37-933e-3b43ae5d121a" "step-c6cce728-296b-55fc-b12d-c3661e00af21"] :activity/first-step "step-f19a2237-84fe-5d2b-8a00-d94a6ea46590" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f19a2237-84fe-5d2b-8a00-d94a6ea46590" :step/id #uuid "f19a2237-84fe-5d2b-8a00-d94a6ea46590" :step/content 17592186335769 :step/next "step-c446fed3-ebea-5ea0-9317-067e792c87d5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c446fed3-ebea-5ea0-9317-067e792c87d5" :step/id #uuid "c446fed3-ebea-5ea0-9317-067e792c87d5" :step/content "question-ac629eca-77ed-5bb7-99e7-49845bdc5d9a" :step/next "step-a13b050c-9930-5688-bdc6-1a08f5d3cefe" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a13b050c-9930-5688-bdc6-1a08f5d3cefe" :step/id #uuid "a13b050c-9930-5688-bdc6-1a08f5d3cefe" :step/content 17592186268887 :step/next "step-97bb20f0-d68d-5f37-933e-3b43ae5d121a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-97bb20f0-d68d-5f37-933e-3b43ae5d121a" :step/id #uuid "97bb20f0-d68d-5f37-933e-3b43ae5d121a" :step/content 17592186268888 :step/next "step-c6cce728-296b-55fc-b12d-c3661e00af21" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c6cce728-296b-55fc-b12d-c3661e00af21" :step/id #uuid "c6cce728-296b-55fc-b12d-c3661e00af21" :step/content 17592186268893 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186367443 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-4a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-a5042924-7265-53a7-81d6-8c89c2f43133" :activity/id #uuid "a5042924-7265-53a7-81d6-8c89c2f43133" :activity/title "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (With Collinearity)" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049557 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-dfff254a-4249-53c2-9587-3cb4f4240ad9" "step-574a82d3-59e7-59b8-9908-4d23793ae75e" "step-e72361cd-94e3-52fd-91cb-f7e78d6bf3a8" "step-29f94980-1925-529b-a3fe-5406bc761b87" "step-8a36cb6a-17f3-5f3e-b938