hat corresponds to $k=16.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.567$ | $2.898$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.583)=0.010.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.583.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3968ba21-a66d-5c03-ace0-c0b9b754c35a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3968ba21-a66d-5c03-ace0-c0b9b754c35a" :answer-field/id #uuid "3968ba21-a66d-5c03-ace0-c0b9b754c35a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5ae8a3a4-26a5-5520-8cb4-3b84f713af3c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf5dd1fe-8c22-5f8f-a950-6a24f329afc7" "answer-fb44504a-e83a-575e-afef-c530129b1033" "answer-e12b5ad6-3955-5bd0-9c42-e5470aeaa64e" "answer-5ae8a3a4-26a5-5520-8cb4-3b84f713af3c" "answer-d0cf561e-ac9e-5a81-9cf0-189ea7937076"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf5dd1fe-8c22-5f8f-a950-6a24f329afc7" :answer/id #uuid "cf5dd1fe-8c22-5f8f-a950-6a24f329afc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.333" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb44504a-e83a-575e-afef-c530129b1033" :answer/id #uuid "fb44504a-e83a-575e-afef-c530129b1033" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.337" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e12b5ad6-3955-5bd0-9c42-e5470aeaa64e" :answer/id #uuid "e12b5ad6-3955-5bd0-9c42-e5470aeaa64e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.746" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ae8a3a4-26a5-5520-8cb4-3b84f713af3c" :answer/id #uuid "5ae8a3a4-26a5-5520-8cb4-3b84f713af3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.583" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0cf561e-ac9e-5a81-9cf0-189ea7937076" :answer/id #uuid "d0cf561e-ac9e-5a81-9cf0-189ea7937076" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.602" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162368 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{17},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X|\\le x)=0.99.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X>x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|X|>x) & =1-P(|X|\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.99 \\\\  & =0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X|>x)=0.01$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X>x)=0.01\\,⟹\\,P(X>x)=0.005.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{17},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=17.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.567$ | $2.898$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.898)=0.005.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.898.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159410 :answer-field/correct 17592186159411 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-d1d03639-0af1-5f5f-aacf-7e8e2248b377" :question/id #uuid "d1d03639-0af1-5f5f-aacf-7e8e2248b377" :question/math-academy-id "q-142731" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{11},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.05.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $10$ | $1.372$ | $1.812$ | $2.228$ | $2.764$ | $3.169$ |\n| $11$ | $1.363$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $2.718$ | $3.106$ |\n| $12$ | $1.356$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $2.681$ | $3.055$ |" :question/worked-solution "![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.05$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X\\ge x)=0.05\\,⟹\\,P(X\\ge x)=0.025.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{11},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=11.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $10$ | $1.372$ | $1.812$ | $2.228$ | $2.764$ | $3.169$ |\n| $11$ | $1.363$ | $1.796$ | $2.201$ | $2.718$ | $3.106$ |\n| $12$ | $1.356$ | $1.782$ | $2.179$ | $2.681$ | $3.055$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.201)=0.025.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.201.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3d5d5597-da21-5f7e-975e-6917a842fec7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3d5d5597-da21-5f7e-975e-6917a842fec7" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d5d5597-da21-5f7e-975e-6917a842fec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-90553719-cd64-556f-aa65-00d0d8df028a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fdb3d4c9-bc0d-5373-bbd4-413f74299d1e" "answer-54111d63-60c2-50d4-a986-69633e4c59d0" "answer-90553719-cd64-556f-aa65-00d0d8df028a" "answer-59de7596-9c87-5e83-86a1-c76e8e1bd1c8" "answer-af653e45-d79f-52af-a984-f7d63322f2a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdb3d4c9-bc0d-5373-bbd4-413f74299d1e" :answer/id #uuid "fdb3d4c9-bc0d-5373-bbd4-413f74299d1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.782" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54111d63-60c2-50d4-a986-69633e4c59d0" :answer/id #uuid "54111d63-60c2-50d4-a986-69633e4c59d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.796" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90553719-cd64-556f-aa65-00d0d8df028a" :answer/id #uuid "90553719-cd64-556f-aa65-00d0d8df028a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.201" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59de7596-9c87-5e83-86a1-c76e8e1bd1c8" :answer/id #uuid "59de7596-9c87-5e83-86a1-c76e8e1bd1c8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.228" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af653e45-d79f-52af-a984-f7d63322f2a5" :answer/id #uuid "af653e45-d79f-52af-a984-f7d63322f2a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3.106" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f847c075-0fad-5c01-8709-91bd3af087df" :question/id #uuid "f847c075-0fad-5c01-8709-91bd3af087df" :question/math-academy-id "q-142732" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{20},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X|\\le x)=0.9.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |" :question/worked-solution "The table shows probabilities in the form $P(X>x).$ So first, we express the desired probability in this form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|X|>x) & =1-P(|X|\\le x) \\\\  & =1-0.9 \\\\  & =0.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.1$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X\\ge x)=0.1\\,⟹\\,P(X\\ge x)=0.05.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{20},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=20.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 1.725)=0.050.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=1.725.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b6291248-be9d-543e-9c1c-4188ae7d9c3b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b6291248-be9d-543e-9c1c-4188ae7d9c3b" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6291248-be9d-543e-9c1c-4188ae7d9c3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2e56ffca-b3ef-5cca-a4ba-5c69226c4603" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac261f3c-c3ad-5082-bbc7-706d5005af7a" "answer-7e27f539-6736-5f9c-b5af-3841001268ef" "answer-2e56ffca-b3ef-5cca-a4ba-5c69226c4603" "answer-cb5bd47e-8fd1-5656-8e8a-b399e9203e04" "answer-2aa7d28d-d199-5375-b98e-a4beff1f130a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac261f3c-c3ad-5082-bbc7-706d5005af7a" :answer/id #uuid "ac261f3c-c3ad-5082-bbc7-706d5005af7a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.323" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e27f539-6736-5f9c-b5af-3841001268ef" :answer/id #uuid "7e27f539-6736-5f9c-b5af-3841001268ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.325" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e56ffca-b3ef-5cca-a4ba-5c69226c4603" :answer/id #uuid "2e56ffca-b3ef-5cca-a4ba-5c69226c4603" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.725" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb5bd47e-8fd1-5656-8e8a-b399e9203e04" :answer/id #uuid "cb5bd47e-8fd1-5656-8e8a-b399e9203e04" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.729" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2aa7d28d-d199-5375-b98e-a4beff1f130a" :answer/id #uuid "2aa7d28d-d199-5375-b98e-a4beff1f130a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.528" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162370 :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{16},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.05.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |" :question/worked-solution "![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.05$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X\\ge x)=0.05\\,⟹\\,P(X\\ge x)=0.025.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{16},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=16.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.567$ | $2.898$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.120)=0.025.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.120.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159422 :answer-field/correct 17592186159424 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-09c96bb5-60ae-50af-af61-489ca13e2d4f" :question/id #uuid "09c96bb5-60ae-50af-af61-489ca13e2d4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142725" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of $x$ that satisfy $P(X\\ge x)=p,$ where $X$ has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom. Given that the random variable $X∼{T}_{19},$ find the value of $x$ such that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.01.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |" :question/worked-solution "![](images/63/635de7075b0d106ffc2a0f15e65ca7a3406a49945591aa5eae59090c74df1398.png)\n\nDue to the symmetry of the student's $t$-distribution, we have that $P(|X|\\ge x)=0.01$ is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2P(X\\ge x)=0.01\\,⟹\\,P(X\\ge x)=0.005.\n$$\n\nNow, since $X∼{T}_{19},$ we focus on the row of the table that corresponds to $k=19.$\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $19$ | $1.328$ | $1.729$ | $2.093$ | $2.539$ | $2.861$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 2.861)=0.005.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our answer is $x=2.861.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6d15ab0d-0b8c-50a7-aa74-8c929db865aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6d15ab0d-0b8c-50a7-aa74-8c929db865aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "6d15ab0d-0b8c-50a7-aa74-8c929db865aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7880b289-4801-5812-a6bb-dd5b4cbd5e03" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ab979eab-5836-596c-a1dc-b301ee130323" "answer-e0d97691-40a5-5b6b-be50-e490aa17e5aa" "answer-2ba1608b-75d7-58e3-a09e-72d201b31cb9" "answer-82448b88-676a-5edb-9b3f-e8f8126177b0" "answer-7880b289-4801-5812-a6bb-dd5b4cbd5e03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab979eab-5836-596c-a1dc-b301ee130323" :answer/id #uuid "ab979eab-5836-596c-a1dc-b301ee130323" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.729" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0d97691-40a5-5b6b-be50-e490aa17e5aa" :answer/id #uuid "e0d97691-40a5-5b6b-be50-e490aa17e5aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.528" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ba1608b-75d7-58e3-a09e-72d201b31cb9" :answer/id #uuid "2ba1608b-75d7-58e3-a09e-72d201b31cb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.539" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82448b88-676a-5edb-9b3f-e8f8126177b0" :answer/id #uuid "82448b88-676a-5edb-9b3f-e8f8126177b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.831" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7880b289-4801-5812-a6bb-dd5b4cbd5e03" :answer/id #uuid "7880b289-4801-5812-a6bb-dd5b4cbd5e03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.861" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163322 :tutorial/content "An important family of distributions in probability and statistics is the **student's $t$-distribution** (or $t$-**distribution**). The diagram below shows the probability density functions for some members of this family.\n\n![](images/08/08b2d66fda68155c6440568508dde075812a7fe0e7a855e1efe175df52830719.png)\n\nEach family member has an associated positive integer parameter $k,$ known as the **degrees of freedom**. \n\nThe probability density function of a student's $t$-distribution is symmetric and bell-shaped, similar to the standard normal distribution. However, $t$-distribution curves have fatter \"tails.\" A consequence is that extreme events (i.e., events far from the mean) are more likely with a $t$-distribution than a standard normal distribution.\n\nTo see how the student's $t$-distribution comes about, consider a large random sample of size $n$\n\n$$\nX_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\ndrawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2.$ According to the central limit theorem, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X}-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\approx N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean. \n\nIn reality, it's unlikely that the population variance $\\sigma^2$ is known, in which case it must be estimated. \n\nRecall that an unbiased estimator for $\\sigma^2,$ known as the sample variance, is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nIf we estimate $\\sigma^2$ using $S^2,$ then it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X}-\\mu}{S/\\sqrt{n}} \\approx T_{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $T_{n-1}$ is a $t$-distributed random variable with $n-1$ degrees of freedom. \n\nThe $t$-distribution is widely used in statistical estimation problems. We'll learn more about the practical applications of this important distribution in future lessons." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163323 :tutorial/content "When working with $t$-distributed random variables, we often want to find a value that gives rise to a specific probability. \n\nFor example, let $X\\sim T_{21}$ be a $t$-distributed random variable with $21$ degrees of freedom. Suppose we want to find the value of $x$ such that \n\n$$\nP(X\\geq x) = 0.01.\n$$\n\nIn other words, we wish to find the $x$-value corresponding to the shaded area shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c3ef041152494232ecb60408580965018132a024c406dcde0506ff519d7ed73.png)\n\nTo compute these probabilities, we use a **percentage points table**. A typical percentage points table for the student's $t$-distribution is shown below.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |\n| $22$ | $1.321$ | $1.717$ | $2.074$ | $2.508$ | $2.819$ |\n| $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nThe rows correspond to different degrees of freedom $k.$ Each cell in the table gives a value $x$ such that \n\n$$\nP(X\\geq x) = p\n$$\n\nfor a particular value of $p.$\n\nSince $X \\sim T_{21},$ we focus on the table row corresponding to $k=21.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.010}$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}2.518}$ | $2.831$ |\n| $22$ | $1.321$ | $1.717$ | $2.074$ | $2.508$ | $2.819$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq {\\color{red}2.518}) = {\\color{blue}0.01}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = 2.518,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d90b9aec3439d4425f9d8998d7795576f5eb714f5bee93784cd556c18b721bd7.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163324 :tutorial/content "The shape of the student's $t$-distribution is similar to that of the standard normal distribution. Are they related, and if so, how?\n\nThe two distributions are indeed related. In fact, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ approaches $N(0,1)$ as $k \\to \\infty.$*\n\nThe diagram below shows the student's $t$-distribution for different degrees of freedom $k$ and the standard normal distribution $N(0,1).$ \n\n![](images/94/942080761ebe53527c36102c5e037eb9ee6ef7cb896aca7df130a4992fc6b0d6.png)\n\nNow, as $k\\to\\infty,$ we have that\n\n- the tails of $T_k$ become slimmer,\n\n- the peak of $T_k$ (i.e., the mode) becomes taller, and\n\n- $T_k$ approaches $N(0,1).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162198 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147415 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f6918689-3a92-5cad-ac6b-0de2903bcad0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f6918689-3a92-5cad-ac6b-0de2903bcad0" :question/id #uuid "f6918689-3a92-5cad-ac6b-0de2903bcad0" :question/math-academy-id "q-166811" :question/problem "Expressed as an improper fraction, the exact area of ​​each wall is$\\,${{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The exact area of  ​each wall is ${(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2} & ={{\\frac{17}{4}\\times \\frac{17}{4}}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{17\\times 17}{4\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}=\\frac{289}{16}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d025d04b-35c6-550c-86ed-3b8dbfd3c675"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d025d04b-35c6-550c-86ed-3b8dbfd3c675" :answer-field/id #uuid "d025d04b-35c6-550c-86ed-3b8dbfd3c675" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-41d5f79a-7cdd-5989-a575-2e2a2e47857b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-41d5f79a-7cdd-5989-a575-2e2a2e47857b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41d5f79a-7cdd-5989-a575-2e2a2e47857b" :answer/id #uuid "41d5f79a-7cdd-5989-a575-2e2a2e47857b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{289}{16}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054858 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ff821477-1720-563f-b73b-9b643857f797"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ff821477-1720-563f-b73b-9b643857f797" :question/id #uuid "ff821477-1720-563f-b73b-9b643857f797" :question/math-academy-id "q-166812" :question/problem "To improve the paint's grip on the wall surface, Mike must dissolve $6.5$ grams of a particular additive in each liter of paint. How much additive is needed if Mike plans to use $2.5$ liters of paint?\n\nExpressed as a decimal, Mike must dissolve$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$grams of additive in $2.5$ liters of paint." :question/worked-solution "To determine the amount of additive that Mike must dissolve in $2.5$ liters of paint, we must multiply $6.5$ by $2.5.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{} \\\\ \\boxed{4}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $6.5$ and there is $1$ decimal place in $2.5.$\nTherefore, their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $1625$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n16\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{25}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nHence, $6.5\\times 2.5=16.25.$\n\nWe conclude that Mike must use $16.25$ grams of additive." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0bb22797-4508-5dc5-b37f-a510cbbdc4f8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0bb22797-4508-5dc5-b37f-a510cbbdc4f8" :answer-field/id #uuid "0bb22797-4508-5dc5-b37f-a510cbbdc4f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e007f3b1-168c-589c-8cea-ade69fdb7fd4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e007f3b1-168c-589c-8cea-ade69fdb7fd4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e007f3b1-168c-589c-8cea-ade69fdb7fd4" :answer/id #uuid "e007f3b1-168c-589c-8cea-ade69fdb7fd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186138368 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d00a784-7b71-55f9-8814-6e64a549eeb5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d00a784-7b71-55f9-8814-6e64a549eeb5" :question/id #uuid "9d00a784-7b71-55f9-8814-6e64a549eeb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-166813" :question/problem "During the first day of painting, Mike uses $1.5$ liters of paint in the morning and $1.32$ liters in the afternoon. How much paint did Mike use that day?\n\nExpressed as a decimal,  Mike used$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of paint." :question/worked-solution "To determine how much paint Mike used, we need to calculate the sum $1.5+1.32.$\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $1.5$ and $2$ decimal places in $1.32.$\n\nTo add the numbers, they both need to have the same number of decimals. Hence, we rewrite the first number so that it has $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n1.5=1.50\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike used $2.82$ liters of paint." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2f909fff-11ff-5d20-9b2f-e687e1ff76cf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2f909fff-11ff-5d20-9b2f-e687e1ff76cf" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f909fff-11ff-5d20-9b2f-e687e1ff76cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-947b69ff-cffc-5c2b-8520-98f7dfd675ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-947b69ff-cffc-5c2b-8520-98f7dfd675ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-947b69ff-cffc-5c2b-8520-98f7dfd675ed" :answer/id #uuid "947b69ff-cffc-5c2b-8520-98f7dfd675ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.82" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054862 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1da853e5-1005-5ae3-a2f4-0c314ac52689"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-1da853e5-1005-5ae3-a2f4-0c314ac52689" :question/id #uuid "1da853e5-1005-5ae3-a2f4-0c314ac52689" :question/math-academy-id "q-166814" :question/problem "All the paint used on Mike's first day came from one can containing $3.78$ liters of paint. How much paint was left in the can after Mike's first day? Express your answer as a decimal.\n\nThere are\n{{field-1}}\nliters of paint were left over." :question/worked-solution "From part 3, we know that Mike has used $2.82$ liters from the first can of paint.\n\nTo determine the amount of paint left in the first can, we need to calculate $3.78-2.82.$\n\nFirst, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $0.96$ liters of paint were left over." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-15294031-e669-5c62-8748-549d11c4708e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-15294031-e669-5c62-8748-549d11c4708e" :answer-field/id #uuid "15294031-e669-5c62-8748-549d11c4708e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b2922c2b-c90a-5c1a-9164-bc221a99c879" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b2922c2b-c90a-5c1a-9164-bc221a99c879"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2922c2b-c90a-5c1a-9164-bc221a99c879" :answer/id #uuid "b2922c2b-c90a-5c1a-9164-bc221a99c879" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073705 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-17da2d52-c4a5-548d-8d46-a96ea5055bec"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-17da2d52-c4a5-548d-8d46-a96ea5055bec" :question/id #uuid "17da2d52-c4a5-548d-8d46-a96ea5055bec" :question/math-academy-id "q-166815" :question/problem "To complete his work, Mike has $4\\,\\frac{4}{5}$ liters of paint. Of this amount, he uses $3\\,\\frac{3}{10}$ liters. How much paint did Mike have left over when he finished? Express your answer as a mixed number in its lowest terms.\n\nMike had$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of paint left." :question/worked-solution "After finishing, Mike had $(4\\,\\frac{4}{5}-3\\,\\frac{3}{10})$ liters of paint left.\n\nWriting $4\\,\\frac{4}{5}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n4\\,\\frac{4}{5}=\\frac{4\\times 5+4}{5}=\\frac{24}{5}.\n$$\n\nWriting $3\\,\\frac{3}{10}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{3}{10}=\\frac{3\\times 10+3}{10}=\\frac{33}{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{5}-\\frac{33}{10}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $10.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{24}{5}$ over a denominator of $10,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{24}{5}=\\frac{24\\times 2}{5\\times 2}=\\frac{48}{10}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{48}{10}-\\frac{33}{10}=\\frac{15}{10}\n$$\n\nSimplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{10}=\\frac{15÷5}{10÷5}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mike had $1\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ liters of paint left." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-98b040f0-50eb-5875-a15e-8411bb429f3c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-98b040f0-50eb-5875-a15e-8411bb429f3c" :answer-field/id #uuid "98b040f0-50eb-5875-a15e-8411bb429f3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-93939cfc-7735-587d-9f9b-25710f4c3ff2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-93939cfc-7735-587d-9f9b-25710f4c3ff2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-93939cfc-7735-587d-9f9b-25710f4c3ff2" :answer/id #uuid "93939cfc-7735-587d-9f9b-25710f4c3ff2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073676 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a137dddd-e843-51d0-beae-620ba6ee763a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a137dddd-e843-51d0-beae-620ba6ee763a" :question/id #uuid "a137dddd-e843-51d0-beae-620ba6ee763a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166816" :question/problem "To complete another job, Mike bought $4$ identical cans of paint, paying a total of $$95.64.$ Estimate the price of each can of paint by rounding the amount of money to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe approximate price of each can of paint is $\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To estimate the price of each can of paint, we need to find an approximation for $95.64÷4.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n95.64\\to 96\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $96$ by $4$ as usual:\n\n$$\n96÷4=24\n$$\n\nTherefore, the approximate price of each can of paint is $$24.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-76af0406-4163-5088-bfc0-7205edc129df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-76af0406-4163-5088-bfc0-7205edc129df" :answer-field/id #uuid "76af0406-4163-5088-bfc0-7205edc129df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f71d0525-5c1a-5770-b1cf-5cb7a58670d0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f71d0525-5c1a-5770-b1cf-5cb7a58670d0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f71d0525-5c1a-5770-b1cf-5cb7a58670d0" :answer/id #uuid "f71d0525-5c1a-5770-b1cf-5cb7a58670d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-a171ca85-4730-530c-a9a0-53a782183f2b" :multistep/id #uuid "a171ca85-4730-530c-a9a0-53a782183f2b" :multistep/context "Mike needs to paint some walls. Each wall has an area of ${(\\frac{17}{4})}^{2}$ square meters." :multistep/steps ["step-aec7697f-aea5-5dca-8a2e-e65000feb195" "step-826f2650-6a6f-51ad-a8b7-8bb2cdea681a" "step-9ccc0773-3284-528e-b8ba-8074d03aa841" "step-a85a5177-b808-53a4-ad57-e81b42e9e3aa" "step-ae8721db-bcc8-5337-aef0-395a67882749" "step-764a4d3c-33b5-5db4-aa32-e4caa4192594"] :multistep/first-step "step-aec7697f-aea5-5dca-8a2e-e65000feb195" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-aec7697f-aea5-5dca-8a2e-e65000feb195" :step/id #uuid "aec7697f-aea5-5dca-8a2e-e65000feb195" :step/content "question-f6918689-3a92-5cad-ac6b-0de2903bcad0" :step/next "step-826f2650-6a6f-51ad-a8b7-8bb2cdea681a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-826f2650-6a6f-51ad-a8b7-8bb2cdea681a" :step/id #uuid "826f2650-6a6f-51ad-a8b7-8bb2cdea681a" :step/content "question-ff821477-1720-563f-b73b-9b643857f797" :step/next "step-9ccc0773-3284-528e-b8ba-8074d03aa841" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9ccc0773-3284-528e-b8ba-8074d03aa841" :step/id #uuid "9ccc0773-3284-528e-b8ba-8074d03aa841" :step/content "question-9d00a784-7b71-55f9-8814-6e64a549eeb5" :step/next "step-a85a5177-b808-53a4-ad57-e81b42e9e3aa" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a85a5177-b808-53a4-ad57-e81b42e9e3aa" :step/id #uuid "a85a5177-b808-53a4-ad57-e81b42e9e3aa" :step/content "question-1da853e5-1005-5ae3-a2f4-0c314ac52689" :step/next "step-ae8721db-bcc8-5337-aef0-395a67882749" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ae8721db-bcc8-5337-aef0-395a67882749" :step/id #uuid "ae8721db-bcc8-5337-aef0-395a67882749" :step/content "question-17da2d52-c4a5-548d-8d46-a96ea5055bec" :step/next "step-764a4d3c-33b5-5db4-aa32-e4caa4192594" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-764a4d3c-33b5-5db4-aa32-e4caa4192594" :step/id #uuid "764a4d3c-33b5-5db4-aa32-e4caa4192594" :step/content "question-a137dddd-e843-51d0-beae-620ba6ee763a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-48841725-96b2-51ae-aae9-4bfafb066075" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "48841725-96b2-51ae-aae9-4bfafb066075" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-a171ca85-4730-530c-a9a0-53a782183f2b" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186048381 17592186049194 17592186049484 17592186049097 17592186050701 17592186050177] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-632fec14-e615-5a75-b6e7-2f7a4be02080" :activity/id #uuid "632fec14-e615-5a75-b6e7-2f7a4be02080" :activity/title "Decimal and Fraction Operations Applied to Commercial Settings" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-ad27ff1f-ba19-5c5d-956f-6f0f53113fa2"] :activity/first-step "step-ad27ff1f-ba19-5c5d-956f-6f0f53113fa2" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-ad27ff1f-ba19-5c5d-956f-6f0f53113fa2" :step/id #uuid "ad27ff1f-ba19-5c5d-956f-6f0f53113fa2" :step/content "assigned-problem-48841725-96b2-51ae-aae9-4bfafb066075" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147474 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-300c5739-f0bd-5d32-a60a-07c8bf92cedc" "question-f7a13f7a-d9b6-5c70-a5f5-bc0989abd19e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-300c5739-f0bd-5d32-a60a-07c8bf92cedc" :question/id #uuid "300c5739-f0bd-5d32-a60a-07c8bf92cedc" :question/math-academy-id "q-1416" :question/problem "In $1990$, there were $5,000$ cell phone subscribers in a city. The number of subscribers increased by $35%$ per year until $2005.$ How many cell-phone subscribers were there in $2005?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of subscribers $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $35%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=5\\,000$ is the initial number of subscribers.\n\n- $r=0.35$ is the growth rate ($35%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in years.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\,000\\cdot (1+0.35)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\,000\\cdot (1.35)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for the number of subscribers after $15$ years, we get\n\n$$\ny(15)=5,000\\cdot (1.35)^{15}≈450\\,792.\n$$\n\nWe round to the nearest whole number because we are talking about people." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-29a2769d-116f-58e7-ae7f-aaabbc05f186"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-29a2769d-116f-58e7-ae7f-aaabbc05f186" :answer-field/id #uuid "29a2769d-116f-58e7-ae7f-aaabbc05f186" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-14107df9-1142-5b13-a4eb-cc0f979fe623" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cec9699d-a223-50f9-941c-c2542b406244" "answer-14107df9-1142-5b13-a4eb-cc0f979fe623" "answer-96687e47-684e-58fb-863d-2da17bbf8bc9" "answer-6de7a497-dd0c-5d56-ac16-d0eaf3570086" "answer-143d216d-e1b6-5b77-b80e-11048d8298ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cec9699d-a223-50f9-941c-c2542b406244" :answer/id #uuid "cec9699d-a223-50f9-941c-c2542b406244" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "441,156" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14107df9-1142-5b13-a4eb-cc0f979fe623" :answer/id #uuid "14107df9-1142-5b13-a4eb-cc0f979fe623" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "450,792" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96687e47-684e-58fb-863d-2da17bbf8bc9" :answer/id #uuid "96687e47-684e-58fb-863d-2da17bbf8bc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "59,058" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6de7a497-dd0c-5d56-ac16-d0eaf3570086" :answer/id #uuid "6de7a497-dd0c-5d56-ac16-d0eaf3570086" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "64,467" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-143d216d-e1b6-5b77-b80e-11048d8298ca" :answer/id #uuid "143d216d-e1b6-5b77-b80e-11048d8298ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "79,379" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f7a13f7a-d9b6-5c70-a5f5-bc0989abd19e" :question/id #uuid "f7a13f7a-d9b6-5c70-a5f5-bc0989abd19e" :question/math-academy-id "q-61916" :question/problem "A watch collector bought a watch for $$50.$ They estimate that the value of the watch increases at a rate of $8%$ every decade. According to this estimate, what will be the price of the watch after $3$ decades? Round your answer to the nearest dollar." :question/worked-solution "The price of the watch $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $8%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nWe have:\n\n- $a=50$ dollars is the original price of the watch.\n\n- $r=0.08$ is the growth rate ($8%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in decades.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =50\\cdot (1+0.08)^{t} \\\\  & =50\\cdot (1.08)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the function at $t=3$ gives the value of the watch after $3$ decades.\n\n$$\ny(3)=50\\cdot (1.08)^{3}≈63\n$$\n\nTherefore, the value of the watch after $3$ decades will be approximately $$63.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-30a9f7e5-72a7-5d8d-87b1-f07b7357b6d3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-30a9f7e5-72a7-5d8d-87b1-f07b7357b6d3" :answer-field/id #uuid "30a9f7e5-72a7-5d8d-87b1-f07b7357b6d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3a06080c-4982-56b7-b5e3-ea683c5488d7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8bf143da-90ae-5cc9-9f95-1d1c3df1b0b0" "answer-b5201925-5100-5ac3-b4cc-f62281b63503" "answer-3a06080c-4982-56b7-b5e3-ea683c5488d7" "answer-529fdd70-21b7-5a61-acff-82b1d5ee51b8" "answer-8a2a7572-9dfa-5124-bbbe-d5a09f71180d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bf143da-90ae-5cc9-9f95-1d1c3df1b0b0" :answer/id #uuid "8bf143da-90ae-5cc9-9f95-1d1c3df1b0b0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$54$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5201925-5100-5ac3-b4cc-f62281b63503" :answer/id #uuid "b5201925-5100-5ac3-b4cc-f62281b63503" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$58$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a06080c-4982-56b7-b5e3-ea683c5488d7" :answer/id #uuid "3a06080c-4982-56b7-b5e3-ea683c5488d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$63$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-529fdd70-21b7-5a61-acff-82b1d5ee51b8" :answer/id #uuid "529fdd70-21b7-5a61-acff-82b1d5ee51b8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$65$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a2a7572-9dfa-5124-bbbe-d5a09f71180d" :answer/id #uuid "8a2a7572-9dfa-5124-bbbe-d5a09f71180d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$68$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147475 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-50e1b98c-c8a1-53cf-8367-e3764f5a2f08" "question-7b3a2ac3-80ac-548f-ab31-d1de79eaa643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-50e1b98c-c8a1-53cf-8367-e3764f5a2f08" :question/id #uuid "50e1b98c-c8a1-53cf-8367-e3764f5a2f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-317613" :question/problem "A video was posted on a streaming platform with $50$ initial views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after the video was posted, the number of views increased by $120%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nAccording to this model, how many views are there estimated to be after $3$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $120%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=50$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.2$ is the growth rate ($120%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after the video was posted\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=50\\cdot (1+1.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=50\\cdot (2.2)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of views after $3$ hours, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=50\\cdot (2.2)^{3}≈532\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of views after $3$ hours is $532.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1232ee90-f900-5499-a331-ceb50d527115"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1232ee90-f900-5499-a331-ceb50d527115" :answer-field/id #uuid "1232ee90-f900-5499-a331-ceb50d527115" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a54c4dd1-562b-5848-ac11-19e631519c9f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-be53aed4-2603-538a-bb13-6ffecb59a0fb" "answer-ab6b2e34-5b0d-511b-9c71-0da9a7de6e76" "answer-a54c4dd1-562b-5848-ac11-19e631519c9f" "answer-99d3b702-8f26-5fec-864d-fc1d46d65b97" "answer-6885483a-aa71-5fea-be44-2ce9914f562d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be53aed4-2603-538a-bb13-6ffecb59a0fb" :answer/id #uuid "be53aed4-2603-538a-bb13-6ffecb59a0fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "198" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab6b2e34-5b0d-511b-9c71-0da9a7de6e76" :answer/id #uuid "ab6b2e34-5b0d-511b-9c71-0da9a7de6e76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "246" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a54c4dd1-562b-5848-ac11-19e631519c9f" :answer/id #uuid "a54c4dd1-562b-5848-ac11-19e631519c9f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "532" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99d3b702-8f26-5fec-864d-fc1d46d65b97" :answer/id #uuid "99d3b702-8f26-5fec-864d-fc1d46d65b97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "700" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6885483a-aa71-5fea-be44-2ce9914f562d" :answer/id #uuid "6885483a-aa71-5fea-be44-2ce9914f562d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "86" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7b3a2ac3-80ac-548f-ab31-d1de79eaa643" :question/id #uuid "7b3a2ac3-80ac-548f-ab31-d1de79eaa643" :question/math-academy-id "q-318202" :question/problem "When a new article was first published on a news website, it had $40$ views. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each hour after publication, the number of views increased by $190%$ of the number of views at the end of the previous hour.\n\nWhich of the following equations best represents this model, where $V$ is the estimated number of views $t$ hours after the article was published, and $t\\le 6?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of views $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $190%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=40$ is the total number of views initially\n\n- $r=1.9$ is the growth rate ($190%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in hours) after publication\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=40\\cdot (1+1.9)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=40\\cdot (2.9)^{t}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8f647258-0007-5333-ac78-26a8426f4858"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8f647258-0007-5333-ac78-26a8426f4858" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f647258-0007-5333-ac78-26a8426f4858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-49751aae-f779-5ca6-9b9e-7a8ffe15ccc7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-961d3d8b-abcf-51c0-a2d4-4c9ea00eafc4" "answer-fffde48d-2021-53c1-b6ab-24b4f090abde" "answer-49751aae-f779-5ca6-9b9e-7a8ffe15ccc7" "answer-77ce4af4-fcc6-55af-ac0a-de8056e6b319" "answer-421513ab-7ba9-53ad-8cc4-202843fff382"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-961d3d8b-abcf-51c0-a2d4-4c9ea00eafc4" :answer/id #uuid "961d3d8b-abcf-51c0-a2d4-4c9ea00eafc4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (0.9)^{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fffde48d-2021-53c1-b6ab-24b4f090abde" :answer/id #uuid "fffde48d-2021-53c1-b6ab-24b4f090abde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (19)^{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-49751aae-f779-5ca6-9b9e-7a8ffe15ccc7" :answer/id #uuid "49751aae-f779-5ca6-9b9e-7a8ffe15ccc7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (2.9)^{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77ce4af4-fcc6-55af-ac0a-de8056e6b319" :answer/id #uuid "77ce4af4-fcc6-55af-ac0a-de8056e6b319" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (3.9)^{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-421513ab-7ba9-53ad-8cc4-202843fff382" :answer/id #uuid "421513ab-7ba9-53ad-8cc4-202843fff382" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "V(t)=40\\cdot (9)^{t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-ec03d21f-b720-5639-be54-4e3700ddbedb" :activity/id #uuid "ec03d21f-b720-5639-be54-4e3700ddbedb" :activity/title "Modeling Exponential Growth With Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048302 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-90b42f16-7d74-5b04-b839-2055a38ea9cf" "step-d0fc5aff-4ed7-58b6-9140-1572b6374187" "step-99c28962-0c9b-59a2-8213-fe4b9b34df2d" "step-3f51c23a-bc05-5e41-b78b-c00a203ad6ee"] :activity/first-step "step-90b42f16-7d74-5b04-b839-2055a38ea9cf" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-90b42f16-7d74-5b04-b839-2055a38ea9cf" :step/id #uuid "90b42f16-7d74-5b04-b839-2055a38ea9cf" :step/content "question-300c5739-f0bd-5d32-a60a-07c8bf92cedc" :step/next "step-d0fc5aff-4ed7-58b6-9140-1572b6374187" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d0fc5aff-4ed7-58b6-9140-1572b6374187" :step/id #uuid "d0fc5aff-4ed7-58b6-9140-1572b6374187" :step/content "question-f7a13f7a-d9b6-5c70-a5f5-bc0989abd19e" :step/next "step-99c28962-0c9b-59a2-8213-fe4b9b34df2d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-99c28962-0c9b-59a2-8213-fe4b9b34df2d" :step/id #uuid "99c28962-0c9b-59a2-8213-fe4b9b34df2d" :step/content "question-50e1b98c-c8a1-53cf-8367-e3764f5a2f08" :step/next "step-3f51c23a-bc05-5e41-b78b-c00a203ad6ee" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3f51c23a-bc05-5e41-b78b-c00a203ad6ee" :step/id #uuid "3f51c23a-bc05-5e41-b78b-c00a203ad6ee" :step/content "question-7b3a2ac3-80ac-548f-ab31-d1de79eaa643" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176064 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7598e646-e70e-5042-8a78-36ec6c081791" "question-51a9e6b4-a8d3-5a57-a795-b4c3e7575128"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175725 :question/problem "Factor $3x^2 - 21x + 30.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $3$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-21x+30 & = \\\\ 3({x}^{2}-7x+10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial $x^2-7x+10$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(10),$ but only the negative factor pairs since the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)$ is negative.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1,-10),\\,(-2,-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-7)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-10) & =-11 & \\times  \\\\ (-2)+(-5) & =-7 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-7x=-2x-5x,$ and factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x+10 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x-5x+10 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x)+(-5x+10) & = \\\\ x(x-2)-5(x-2) & = \\\\ (x-5)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n3\\left(x^2-7x+10\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n3(x-5)(x-2)\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175949 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b8115771-d0c0-58ad-81dd-b9545f4b8d28"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175797 :question/problem "Factor the expression $-2x^2-22x-48.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $-2$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2{x}^{2}-22x-48 & = \\\\ -2({x}^{2}+11x+24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial $x^2+11x+24$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(24)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1,24),\\,(2,12),\\,(3,8),\\,(4,6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(11)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+24 & =25 & \\times  \\\\ 2+12 & =14 & \\times  \\\\ 3+8 & =11 & ✓ \\\\ 4+6 & =10 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $11x=3x+8x,$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n-2\\left(x^2+11x+24\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\left(x^2+3x+8x+24\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\left((x^2+3x)+(8x+24)\\right)&=\\[5pt]\n-2\\Bigl(x(x+3)+8(x+3)\\Bigr)&=\\[5pt]\n-2(x+8)(x+3)\n\t}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7598e646-e70e-5042-8a78-36ec6c081791" :question/id #uuid "7598e646-e70e-5042-8a78-36ec6c081791" :question/math-academy-id "q-32107" :question/problem "If $5{b}^{2}-15b+10=5(b+p)(b+q),$ then $p+q=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out $5$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{b}^{2}-15b+10 & =5({b}^{2}-3b+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${b}^{2}-3b+2$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(2).$ We will want to find the factor pair that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-3),$ so we will consider the negative factor pairs in particular:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & \\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-2) & =-3 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-3b=-b-2b$ and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}^{2}-3b+2 & = \\\\ {b}^{2}-b-2b+2 & = \\\\ ({b}^{2}-b)+(-2b+2) & = \\\\ b(b-1)-2(b-1) & = \\\\ (b-2)(b-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({b}^{2}-3b+2) & =5(b-2)(b-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=-2,q=-1,$ and $p+q=-3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-06764b2b-0fcb-5201-9043-354b15b5afa9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-06764b2b-0fcb-5201-9043-354b15b5afa9" :answer-field/id #uuid "06764b2b-0fcb-5201-9043-354b15b5afa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7f090948-7631-5696-93e9-27c3f569baa7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f090948-7631-5696-93e9-27c3f569baa7" "answer-d3062eb8-1be4-55c1-abe5-0531007f28e9" "answer-a936416f-f189-5f46-bd0f-f7f48beaf553" "answer-9bdcfe25-e05c-5e4e-b69a-8716e49777a9" "answer-5b107508-c497-5022-876e-eecbf2e11f49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f090948-7631-5696-93e9-27c3f569baa7" :answer/id #uuid "7f090948-7631-5696-93e9-27c3f569baa7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3062eb8-1be4-55c1-abe5-0531007f28e9" :answer/id #uuid "d3062eb8-1be4-55c1-abe5-0531007f28e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a936416f-f189-5f46-bd0f-f7f48beaf553" :answer/id #uuid "a936416f-f189-5f46-bd0f-f7f48beaf553" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9bdcfe25-e05c-5e4e-b69a-8716e49777a9" :answer/id #uuid "9bdcfe25-e05c-5e4e-b69a-8716e49777a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b107508-c497-5022-876e-eecbf2e11f49" :answer/id #uuid "5b107508-c497-5022-876e-eecbf2e11f49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-51a9e6b4-a8d3-5a57-a795-b4c3e7575128" :question/id #uuid "51a9e6b4-a8d3-5a57-a795-b4c3e7575128" :question/math-academy-id "q-2855" :question/problem "$5{t}^{2}-15t-20=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out $5$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{t}^{2}-15t-20 & =5({t}^{2}-3t-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${t}^{2}-3t-4$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(-4).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-4) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 4 \\\\ 2 & \\times (-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-4) & =-3 & ✓ \\\\ (-1)+4 & =3 & \\times  \\\\ 2+(-2) & =0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $-3t=t-4t$ and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3t-4 & = \\\\ {t}^{2}+t-4t-4 & = \\\\ ({t}^{2}+t)+(-4t-4) & = \\\\ t(t+1)-4(t+1) & = \\\\ (t-4)(t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({t}^{2}-3t-4) & =5(t-4)(t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-94c06265-2366-51a7-b0d0-03e55f741385"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-94c06265-2366-51a7-b0d0-03e55f741385" :answer-field/id #uuid "94c06265-2366-51a7-b0d0-03e55f741385" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9b86139f-59b5-5233-bc2a-d13f5dba4489" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b9e27368-2343-5732-958d-8a429ac47df7" "answer-492942ee-22f4-59db-934a-e22733b6184d" "answer-52b70ec8-bf60-5ca2-9500-bf2667045ba3" "answer-ef378e59-9ad9-542b-aa16-7399d5fbff76" "answer-9b86139f-59b5-5233-bc2a-d13f5dba4489"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9e27368-2343-5732-958d-8a429ac47df7" :answer/id #uuid "b9e27368-2343-5732-958d-8a429ac47df7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5t+2)(t-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-492942ee-22f4-59db-934a-e22733b6184d" :answer/id #uuid "492942ee-22f4-59db-934a-e22733b6184d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5t-4)(t+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52b70ec8-bf60-5ca2-9500-bf2667045ba3" :answer/id #uuid "52b70ec8-bf60-5ca2-9500-bf2667045ba3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(t+2)(t-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef378e59-9ad9-542b-aa16-7399d5fbff76" :answer/id #uuid "ef378e59-9ad9-542b-aa16-7399d5fbff76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(t+4)(t-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b86139f-59b5-5233-bc2a-d13f5dba4489" :answer/id #uuid "9b86139f-59b5-5233-bc2a-d13f5dba4489" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5(t-4)(t+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176463 :question/problem "If $-3{a}^{2}+30a-72=-3(a+p)(a+q),$ then $p+q=$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $-3$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{a}^{2}+30a-72 & =-3({a}^{2}-10a+24)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${a}^{2}-10a+24$ in parentheses.\n\nFirst, we find the factor pairs of the constant term $(24).$ We will want to find the factor pair that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10),$ so we will consider the negative factor pairs in particular:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1) & \\times (-24) \\\\ (-2) & \\times (-12) \\\\ (-3) & \\times (-8) \\\\ (-4) & \\times (-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-10):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)+(-24) & =-25 & \\times  \\\\ (-2)+(-12) & =-14 & \\times  \\\\ (-3)+(-8) & =-11 & \\times  \\\\ (-4)+(-6) & =-10 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified in the previous step, write the linear term as $-10a=-4a-6a,$ and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({a}^{2}-10a+24) & = \\\\ -3({a}^{2}-4a-6a+24) & = \\\\ -3(a(a-4)-6(a-4)) & = \\\\ -3(a-4)(a-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-4,q=-6,$ and $p+q=-10.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174918 :answer-field/correct 17592186174920 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b8115771-d0c0-58ad-81dd-b9545f4b8d28" :question/id #uuid "b8115771-d0c0-58ad-81dd-b9545f4b8d28" :question/math-academy-id "q-80173" :question/problem "Factor $6-3x-3{x}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can factor out a $(-3)$ from each of the terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6-3x-3{x}^{2} & =-3({x}^{2}+x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the polynomial ${x}^{2}+x-2$ in parentheses.\n\nWe start by listing the factor pairs of the constant term $(-2).$ The constant term is negative, so that means one of the factors is negative and the other is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & \\times (-2) \\\\ (-1) & \\times 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find which factor pair adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+(-2) & =-1 & \\times  \\\\ (-1)+(2) & =1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the factor pair identified above, we write the linear term as $x=-x+2x$ and then factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({x}^{2}+x-2) & = \\\\ -3({x}^{2}-x+2x-2) & = \\\\ -3(({x}^{2}-x)+(2x-2)) & = \\\\ -3(x(x-1)+2(x-1)) & = \\\\ -3(x+2)(x-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-37ecc6ff-8871-56b2-aab9-81dfb39a048c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-37ecc6ff-8871-56b2-aab9-81dfb39a048c" :answer-field/id #uuid "37ecc6ff-8871-56b2-aab9-81dfb39a048c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0472e445-7f02-503d-8b15-9cb91772ed43" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b601254e-067f-525c-b190-c1e96f078bfd" "answer-9c579a24-2a9d-597c-bcee-586175e4ecd7" "answer-ae8abbbd-95f4-5109-a120-3ff6ea915413" "answer-0472e445-7f02-503d-8b15-9cb91772ed43" "answer-7be4e3a3-34cb-572b-8974-2b87a20cfbb4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b601254e-067f-525c-b190-c1e96f078bfd" :answer/id #uuid "b601254e-067f-525c-b190-c1e96f078bfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(3x+2)(x-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c579a24-2a9d-597c-bcee-586175e4ecd7" :answer/id #uuid "9c579a24-2a9d-597c-bcee-586175e4ecd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+2)(x-3)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae8abbbd-95f4-5109-a120-3ff6ea915413" :answer/id #uuid "ae8abbbd-95f4-5109-a120-3ff6ea915413" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-(3x-2)(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0472e445-7f02-503d-8b15-9cb91772ed43" :answer/id #uuid "0472e445-7f02-503d-8b15-9cb91772ed43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3(x+2)(x-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7be4e3a3-34cb-572b-8974-2b87a20cfbb4" :answer/id #uuid "7be4e3a3-34cb-572b-8974-2b87a20cfbb4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3(x-2)(x+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176965 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to factor the polynomial \n\n$$\n8x^2 + 24x + 16.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks tricky because the coefficients are rather large! \n\nHowever, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $8.$ If we first factor out an $8$ from each of the terms, then we can cut the factoring down to something more manageable:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t8x^2 + 24x + 16 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t8(x^2 + 3x + 2)\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the polynomial $x^2 + 3x + 2$ in the parentheses.\n\nThe constant term $(2)$ has a single factor pair, namely\n\n$$\n(1, 2).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the numbers in this factor pair indeed sum to the linear term's coefficient $(1+2=3).$ \n\nSo, we can write the linear term as $3x=x+2x$ and factor as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x+2 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+x+2x+2 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x)+(2x+2) & = \\\\ x(x+1)+2(x+1) & = \\\\ (x+2)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}+24x+16 & = \\\\ 8({x}^{2}+3x+2) & = \\\\ 8(x+2)(x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176126 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186062185 :answer-field/choices 17592186062186] [:db/retract 17592186062185 :answer-field/choices 17592186062187] [:db/retract 17592186062185 :answer-field/choices 17592186062188] [:db/retract 17592186062185 :answer-field/choices 17592186062189] [:db/retract 17592186062185 :answer-field/choices 17592186062190] {:db/id 17592186064372 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-5b15db96-5f99-5a26-80eb-fe738443b083" "question-bcf0d4e1-9821-591f-a3fd-6de72f482ee3" "question-2dde4916-a480-580f-9e20-db5c7a8b1aba" "question-17fd30dd-fe66-5909-8dae-293b86cae983"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064373 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7140d279-862c-5a41-be5a-097813177226" "question-f8bf196f-ddea-5382-bd16-d659e05ea09d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064375 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4f2920eb-bbfa-5091-822d-b1cc3cc2b1d5" "question-fb99c2d9-3bf4-5523-9da0-10f469bef9ed" "question-c26c64d2-f1a3-5c4d-8afc-7e98e275ec7f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064374 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a2135fd1-33ca-5d7e-9987-278b24125900" "question-850324c7-bb98-55b1-99ad-faf0afddb108" "question-8fede564-db3c-5749-8f57-8bfacce22a82"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064688 :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc27a817900f1e7bef61f73da6bbdb3aaae4e30d59490dce4395416d98f4edae.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $2\\text{ m/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $10\\text{ m}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $2\\,\\text{m/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.$ Also, when the sides are $10\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=10.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(2)\\cdot 2(10)=40{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $40\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186062185 :answer-field/correct "answer-bd5aa18a-f522-5524-af5f-96af2c3c7291" :answer-field/choices ["answer-89c12662-6dd9-59fc-9fe9-236a35f3e35f" "answer-ded90f3d-f805-5c66-a067-ce35f5768a33" "answer-c9ac6247-20c9-56f4-95b4-54c90039906a" "answer-ac8b9716-e2de-5c53-a065-b7d01beb7a5e" "answer-bd5aa18a-f522-5524-af5f-96af2c3c7291"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89c12662-6dd9-59fc-9fe9-236a35f3e35f" :answer/id #uuid "89c12662-6dd9-59fc-9fe9-236a35f3e35f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20\\text{ m}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ded90f3d-f805-5c66-a067-ce35f5768a33" :answer/id #uuid "ded90f3d-f805-5c66-a067-ce35f5768a33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9ac6247-20c9-56f4-95b4-54c90039906a" :answer/id #uuid "c9ac6247-20c9-56f4-95b4-54c90039906a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac8b9716-e2de-5c53-a065-b7d01beb7a5e" :answer/id #uuid "ac8b9716-e2de-5c53-a065-b7d01beb7a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40\\text{ m}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd5aa18a-f522-5524-af5f-96af2c3c7291" :answer/id #uuid "bd5aa18a-f522-5524-af5f-96af2c3c7291" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "40{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5b15db96-5f99-5a26-80eb-fe738443b083" :question/id #uuid "5b15db96-5f99-5a26-80eb-fe738443b083" :question/math-academy-id "q-314490" :question/problem "The sides (in meters) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to fourteen times the rate of increase of the sides. What is the area $A$ of the square at that moment?\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}${\\text{ m}}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=14\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 14\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 14 & =2x \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $x=7\\text{ m}$ and the area of the square is\n\n$$\nA={7}^{2}=49{\\text{ m}}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9865f5a2-d5d6-5e7c-9f42-56cea805a3bc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9865f5a2-d5d6-5e7c-9f42-56cea805a3bc" :answer-field/id #uuid "9865f5a2-d5d6-5e7c-9f42-56cea805a3bc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3f767d27-f5ed-5560-a033-3e945bc4cde9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f767d27-f5ed-5560-a033-3e945bc4cde9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f767d27-f5ed-5560-a033-3e945bc4cde9" :answer/id #uuid "3f767d27-f5ed-5560-a033-3e945bc4cde9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bcf0d4e1-9821-591f-a3fd-6de72f482ee3" :question/id #uuid "bcf0d4e1-9821-591f-a3fd-6de72f482ee3" :question/math-academy-id "q-344455" :question/problem "![](images/e1/e1fd2786b6a375a4c1d0151b38ad5daf222631ee33e96bc02ada00ef67e051ce.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $6\\text{ m/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the side is $5\\,\\text{m}$ long?\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{m/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6.$ Also, when the sides are $5\\,\\text{m},$ we have $x=5.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\cdot 2(5) \\\\  & =60{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at\n$60\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99ac03b7-904c-5b89-9b95-3862238cdcb2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99ac03b7-904c-5b89-9b95-3862238cdcb2" :answer-field/id #uuid "99ac03b7-904c-5b89-9b95-3862238cdcb2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-727566af-2b70-549e-be78-bf01a429c02f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-727566af-2b70-549e-be78-bf01a429c02f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-727566af-2b70-549e-be78-bf01a429c02f" :answer/id #uuid "727566af-2b70-549e-be78-bf01a429c02f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2dde4916-a480-580f-9e20-db5c7a8b1aba" :question/id #uuid "2dde4916-a480-580f-9e20-db5c7a8b1aba" :question/math-academy-id "q-115923" :question/problem "The sides (in inches) of a square are increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the square is numerically equal to eleven times the rate of increase of the sides. What is the area of the square at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=11\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 11\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 11 & =2x \\\\ x & =5.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, at that particular moment, we have $x=5.5\\text{ in}$ and the area of the square is\n\n$$\nA={5.5}^{2}=30.25{\\text{ in}}^{2}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f56de714-5cdc-5f19-b3b0-cef270a1eb6e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f56de714-5cdc-5f19-b3b0-cef270a1eb6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "f56de714-5cdc-5f19-b3b0-cef270a1eb6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dd24220e-3876-55fa-81e4-4fd007c1ad14" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1f35e7ed-cb3e-54ad-9e21-254f09984942" "answer-dd24220e-3876-55fa-81e4-4fd007c1ad14" "answer-0843cafa-6c1b-588f-a4b6-400d353520a7" "answer-74367f1d-8b69-58fd-86eb-c4d6b8b5fc47" "answer-eeeeed9f-a43d-552c-8c0e-e7e72a8e5855"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f35e7ed-cb3e-54ad-9e21-254f09984942" :answer/id #uuid "1f35e7ed-cb3e-54ad-9e21-254f09984942" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30.05{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd24220e-3876-55fa-81e4-4fd007c1ad14" :answer/id #uuid "dd24220e-3876-55fa-81e4-4fd007c1ad14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30.25{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0843cafa-6c1b-588f-a4b6-400d353520a7" :answer/id #uuid "0843cafa-6c1b-588f-a4b6-400d353520a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74367f1d-8b69-58fd-86eb-c4d6b8b5fc47" :answer/id #uuid "74367f1d-8b69-58fd-86eb-c4d6b8b5fc47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "32.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eeeeed9f-a43d-552c-8c0e-e7e72a8e5855" :answer/id #uuid "eeeeed9f-a43d-552c-8c0e-e7e72a8e5855" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "35.2{\\text{ in}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-17fd30dd-fe66-5909-8dae-293b86cae983" :question/id #uuid "17fd30dd-fe66-5909-8dae-293b86cae983" :question/math-academy-id "q-73309" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b6462df728eefef5a942fb409153dc1ba67d4bc065afb281f86b1bd9ac751d20.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $7\\,\\text{mm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $12\\,\\text{mm}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $7\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=7.$ Also, when the sides are $12\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $x=12.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(7)\\cdot 2(12)=168{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $168\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ae71706-c0f4-5bda-827d-bb7d8c83ba6e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ae71706-c0f4-5bda-827d-bb7d8c83ba6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ae71706-c0f4-5bda-827d-bb7d8c83ba6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-99ef838f-ba04-56c6-8ad7-d24b062b203d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-823f29bb-0f89-5de2-8494-842a1f5859f3" "answer-ab62f859-7f30-514a-8597-c0b385aa0a14" "answer-c32beff5-1b79-5499-b695-6642a4a93125" "answer-99ef838f-ba04-56c6-8ad7-d24b062b203d" "answer-b0487d7e-a1c5-586f-87a9-4ac684e34472"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-823f29bb-0f89-5de2-8494-842a1f5859f3" :answer/id #uuid "823f29bb-0f89-5de2-8494-842a1f5859f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "100\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab62f859-7f30-514a-8597-c0b385aa0a14" :answer/id #uuid "ab62f859-7f30-514a-8597-c0b385aa0a14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "108\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c32beff5-1b79-5499-b695-6642a4a93125" :answer/id #uuid "c32beff5-1b79-5499-b695-6642a4a93125" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "128\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99ef838f-ba04-56c6-8ad7-d24b062b203d" :answer/id #uuid "99ef838f-ba04-56c6-8ad7-d24b062b203d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "168\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0487d7e-a1c5-586f-87a9-4ac684e34472" :answer/id #uuid "b0487d7e-a1c5-586f-87a9-4ac684e34472" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "98\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064811 :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $10{\\text{ in}}^{2}\\text{/hour}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in hours." :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/d7/d713fe1a9286ec36d413bda32cce5ea4327003e6cc4f8a40a45cb945166d68c1.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\text{ in}{}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{5}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186062923 :answer-field/correct 17592186062928 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-7140d279-862c-5a41-be5a-097813177226" :question/id #uuid "7140d279-862c-5a41-be5a-097813177226" :question/math-academy-id "q-48338" :question/problem "The area of a square increases at a rate of $25{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the lengths of the sides of the square increasing when the sides are $2\\text{ cm}$ long?" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/a2/a2bd105cbf7aa6fc2af8b2189e63122acfbb0bcf70b007cd35efd8492d273dca.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =25\\text{ cm}{}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{25}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $2\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=2.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{25}{2\\cdot (2)}=\\frac{25}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lengths of the sides are increasing at a rate of $\\frac{25}{4}\\,\\text{cm/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d6d5917d-5bf7-5e58-a475-dcbda538b4bd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d6d5917d-5bf7-5e58-a475-dcbda538b4bd" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d5917d-5bf7-5e58-a475-dcbda538b4bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fbf7b751-e003-5879-bd97-6773f0dd0b0b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2456cc6c-5e4d-5920-b972-00d894004b75" "answer-079398ab-7ac1-54ec-8313-d5341767d2f8" "answer-342916e8-5acb-52d6-a2fa-48b79d7d1f2e" "answer-fbf7b751-e003-5879-bd97-6773f0dd0b0b" "answer-8a0d9c47-25a9-5034-b11f-37b02fa593f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2456cc6c-5e4d-5920-b972-00d894004b75" :answer/id #uuid "2456cc6c-5e4d-5920-b972-00d894004b75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\text{ cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-079398ab-7ac1-54ec-8313-d5341767d2f8" :answer/id #uuid "079398ab-7ac1-54ec-8313-d5341767d2f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\text{ cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-342916e8-5acb-52d6-a2fa-48b79d7d1f2e" :answer/id #uuid "342916e8-5acb-52d6-a2fa-48b79d7d1f2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\text{ cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbf7b751-e003-5879-bd97-6773f0dd0b0b" :answer/id #uuid "fbf7b751-e003-5879-bd97-6773f0dd0b0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}\\text{ cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a0d9c47-25a9-5034-b11f-37b02fa593f9" :answer/id #uuid "8a0d9c47-25a9-5034-b11f-37b02fa593f9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\text{ cm/s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f8bf196f-ddea-5382-bd16-d659e05ea09d" :question/id #uuid "f8bf196f-ddea-5382-bd16-d659e05ea09d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344525" :question/problem "The area of a square is decreasing at a rate of $48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the side of ​​the square decreasing when the side is $4\\text{ cm}$ long?\n\nThe side is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/10/10e47419775a585e10e732f482ee279bba49d205ee12959cd80c8d33924accbf.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-48\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-48 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{48}{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{48}{2\\cdot 4}=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the side is decreasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ea4e14ee-7928-5c6b-b973-9fa55f6b887b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ea4e14ee-7928-5c6b-b973-9fa55f6b887b" :answer-field/id #uuid "ea4e14ee-7928-5c6b-b973-9fa55f6b887b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9c85fd9e-d734-57a2-b3b3-5f7387d158d9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9c85fd9e-d734-57a2-b3b3-5f7387d158d9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c85fd9e-d734-57a2-b3b3-5f7387d158d9" :answer/id #uuid "9c85fd9e-d734-57a2-b3b3-5f7387d158d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4f2920eb-bbfa-5091-822d-b1cc3cc2b1d5" :question/id #uuid "4f2920eb-bbfa-5091-822d-b1cc3cc2b1d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344607" :question/problem "The area of a square decreases at a rate of $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$ At what rate is the diagonal of the square decreasing when the diagonal is $20\\text{ cm}$ long?\n\nThe diagonal is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square decreases at a rate of $10{\\text{ cm}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-10.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $20\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $h=20.$ Substituting this information into the above expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 20 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{10}{20} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.5\\text{ cm}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal is decreasing at $0.5\\text{ cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a07a90e1-3f2d-527c-95e4-9e7865dfa008"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a07a90e1-3f2d-527c-95e4-9e7865dfa008" :answer-field/id #uuid "a07a90e1-3f2d-527c-95e4-9e7865dfa008" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d9218b54-f286-5305-8d91-cddb1cc2cb56" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d9218b54-f286-5305-8d91-cddb1cc2cb56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9218b54-f286-5305-8d91-cddb1cc2cb56" :answer/id #uuid "d9218b54-f286-5305-8d91-cddb1cc2cb56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fb99c2d9-3bf4-5523-9da0-10f469bef9ed" :question/id #uuid "fb99c2d9-3bf4-5523-9da0-10f469bef9ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-116052" :question/problem "![](images/88/88adbcd9d9bd93da5386546b517d765ef6ccd584ae690e7d4ce0d3047cdfc48c.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ cm}\\text{/s},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-7\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-7\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-28\\text{ cm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $28\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f8252af9-6724-51ce-b683-1b74d5d5cb10"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f8252af9-6724-51ce-b683-1b74d5d5cb10" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8252af9-6724-51ce-b683-1b74d5d5cb10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-131831ae-535d-565d-82c5-5eeef54c306a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0798d16f-f5b7-5492-a8d2-0850252014fd" "answer-bb402b80-0491-5b26-9210-7d2228540603" "answer-6070339a-825a-5fe2-880f-267d7547184e" "answer-131831ae-535d-565d-82c5-5eeef54c306a" "answer-865d0e1a-3c0c-5af4-bcfa-841746f13cb7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0798d16f-f5b7-5492-a8d2-0850252014fd" :answer/id #uuid "0798d16f-f5b7-5492-a8d2-0850252014fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb402b80-0491-5b26-9210-7d2228540603" :answer/id #uuid "bb402b80-0491-5b26-9210-7d2228540603" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "21\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6070339a-825a-5fe2-880f-267d7547184e" :answer/id #uuid "6070339a-825a-5fe2-880f-267d7547184e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "24\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-131831ae-535d-565d-82c5-5eeef54c306a" :answer/id #uuid "131831ae-535d-565d-82c5-5eeef54c306a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-865d0e1a-3c0c-5af4-bcfa-841746f13cb7" :answer/id #uuid "865d0e1a-3c0c-5af4-bcfa-841746f13cb7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\text{ cm}/\\text{s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c26c64d2-f1a3-5c4d-8afc-7e98e275ec7f" :question/id #uuid "c26c64d2-f1a3-5c4d-8afc-7e98e275ec7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-344610" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4851331d4899976fb59bb469f19604716fd33235470d24a725abbb26e886279.png)\n\nThe diagonals of a square are decreasing at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate is the perimeter of the square decreasing?\n\nThe perimeter is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}\\,h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}\\,h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $7\\sqrt{2}\\text{ m}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-7\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}=(-7\\sqrt{2})(2\\sqrt{2})=-28\\text{ m/min}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $28\\,\\text{m/min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a52d0c63-7cee-55f6-be50-18ed8125a84f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a52d0c63-7cee-55f6-be50-18ed8125a84f" :answer-field/id #uuid "a52d0c63-7cee-55f6-be50-18ed8125a84f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-42afc9a4-d297-5d8a-b35b-dccdfacc0dc9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-42afc9a4-d297-5d8a-b35b-dccdfacc0dc9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42afc9a4-d297-5d8a-b35b-dccdfacc0dc9" :answer/id #uuid "42afc9a4-d297-5d8a-b35b-dccdfacc0dc9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a2135fd1-33ca-5d7e-9987-278b24125900" :question/id #uuid "a2135fd1-33ca-5d7e-9987-278b24125900" :question/math-academy-id "q-344664" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef685549b562c570914bc8b5b5066cb621e42c8536586b37467afc6c1eb6c194.png)\n\nAn adjustable square skylight is being expanded as part of a building renovation. The area of the skylight is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals increasing when each diagonal measures $18\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe diagonal is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the area of the square skylight, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is increasing at a rate of $27{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=27.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=18.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 27 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{27}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =1.5\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the skylight is increasing at a rate of \n$1.5\\text{ mm/s}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-540ea305-c7f1-5022-950d-9653c1a23171"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-540ea305-c7f1-5022-950d-9653c1a23171" :answer-field/id #uuid "540ea305-c7f1-5022-950d-9653c1a23171" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1" :answer/id #uuid "c482fa62-bdad-55bc-8f75-9c1122d494b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-850324c7-bb98-55b1-99ad-faf0afddb108" :question/id #uuid "850324c7-bb98-55b1-99ad-faf0afddb108" :question/math-academy-id "q-2777" :question/problem "![](images/19/199f1c7e18eb0c25bf7caafb8e2e71fa3e0165af81083ea57c1fc16a74935ab3.png)\n\nA square's diagonals decrease at the rate of  $2\\text{ m}\\text{/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square decreasing when the diagonals are $3\\text{ m}$ long each?" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2\\,\\text{m/min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=3.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\cdot 3=-6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-09e91639-868e-5146-8866-35d968fb2a9b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-09e91639-868e-5146-8866-35d968fb2a9b" :answer-field/id #uuid "09e91639-868e-5146-8866-35d968fb2a9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-762d9397-20d2-55c1-bf20-cc6031d3df47" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cdf8fb62-6cae-52c3-b37d-05d7c31d9523" "answer-8ec08695-a478-5d1f-a7ad-7cc9d9bedfb5" "answer-52f9369b-e6c7-5f61-8fc5-c873059be79e" "answer-762d9397-20d2-55c1-bf20-cc6031d3df47" "answer-df357ea5-8c84-50a9-8a37-c8ef860e991f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cdf8fb62-6cae-52c3-b37d-05d7c31d9523" :answer/id #uuid "cdf8fb62-6cae-52c3-b37d-05d7c31d9523" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8ec08695-a478-5d1f-a7ad-7cc9d9bedfb5" :answer/id #uuid "8ec08695-a478-5d1f-a7ad-7cc9d9bedfb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52f9369b-e6c7-5f61-8fc5-c873059be79e" :answer/id #uuid "52f9369b-e6c7-5f61-8fc5-c873059be79e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-762d9397-20d2-55c1-bf20-cc6031d3df47" :answer/id #uuid "762d9397-20d2-55c1-bf20-cc6031d3df47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df357ea5-8c84-50a9-8a37-c8ef860e991f" :answer/id #uuid "df357ea5-8c84-50a9-8a37-c8ef860e991f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8fede564-db3c-5749-8f57-8bfacce22a82" :question/id #uuid "8fede564-db3c-5749-8f57-8bfacce22a82" :question/math-academy-id "q-48375" :question/problem "![](images/97/97a7d465f8412c88367ed3cf148af3ce387441284c10e3f84888c0186fa597ce.png)\n\nPeter wants to cut a piece of square-shaped glass while maintaining its shape. The area of this glass is decreasing at a rate of $1.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$ At what rate are the diagonals of the glass decreasing if the length of these diagonals is $50\\text{ in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $A$ decreases at a rate of $1.5{\\text{ in}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=50.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{50}\\cdot (-1.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1.5}{50} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-0.03\\,\\text{in}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the diagonals of the glass are decreasing at a rate of $0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a33fd242-f46e-5e85-8083-9fc0a177d7df"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a33fd242-f46e-5e85-8083-9fc0a177d7df" :answer-field/id #uuid "a33fd242-f46e-5e85-8083-9fc0a177d7df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-10a41103-4584-501c-b5c9-46b575346904" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10a41103-4584-501c-b5c9-46b575346904" "answer-ab8e0339-f5c6-51f4-989a-0cdb574502ea" "answer-a772f7bf-13d7-5a11-b09f-eb30c2bf7b07" "answer-cd3e88a9-534e-5c2c-95bc-3fa313944feb" "answer-c44d2824-b8b1-5867-82fe-eb017c5a781d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10a41103-4584-501c-b5c9-46b575346904" :answer/id #uuid "10a41103-4584-501c-b5c9-46b575346904" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.03\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab8e0339-f5c6-51f4-989a-0cdb574502ea" :answer/id #uuid "ab8e0339-f5c6-51f4-989a-0cdb574502ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.05\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a772f7bf-13d7-5a11-b09f-eb30c2bf7b07" :answer/id #uuid "a772f7bf-13d7-5a11-b09f-eb30c2bf7b07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.125\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd3e88a9-534e-5c2c-95bc-3fa313944feb" :answer/id #uuid "cd3e88a9-534e-5c2c-95bc-3fa313944feb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c44d2824-b8b1-5867-82fe-eb017c5a781d" :answer/id #uuid "c44d2824-b8b1-5867-82fe-eb017c5a781d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2\\text{ in}\\text{/min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065380 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the lengths of the sides of a square are growing at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, as shown below.\n\n![](images/6e/6e791c7c33636ef3b2b798fde58825cf4cf1f76e3b6abdd2d92317aa555e1420.png)\n\nIf the square is growing, then its area is also increasing. Our question is, \"*what is the rate at which the area of the square is increasing*?\" In other words, if $A$ is the area of the square at time $t$, then what is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}?\n$$\n\nLet $x$ be the length of the side of the square. We know that the relationship between the area $A$ and side length $x$ is given by\n\n$$\nA = x^2.\n$$\n\nUsing implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $x$ grows at a **rate** of $2\\,\\textrm{cm/s},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t} = 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our expression for $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 2 \\cdot 2x =4x.\n$$\n\nAnd that's it. We're done!\n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $x$ at that moment into the above.\n\nFor example, to work out the rate of change of area when $t=2$ seconds, we substitute the corresponding value of $x$ into the formula. When $t=2$ we have $x=4$ (from the diagram) and therefore\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4(4) = 16\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186065382 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the rate of change of the diagonal of a square is known, and we're interested in finding the rate of change of some other quantity. For example, can we calculate the rate of change of the area of a square given the rate of change of its diagonal?\n\nBefore we get into that, let's recall a few facts about squares. Consider the square $ABCD$ with sides of length $x$ and diagonals of length $h.$\n\n![](images/0c/0cc3f0cd81cae3bab92ec6c3826f64b7e54e0af61cc32a000569afe5171a27dc.png)\n\nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem, we know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ 2{x}^{2} & ={h}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the area $A$ of a square is given by $A = x^2.$ So, since $x^2 = \\dfrac{h^2}{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{h^2}{2}.\n$$\n\nThis formula will prove particularly handy in the next example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064454 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186169107 :answer-field/choices 17592186169108] [:db/retract 17592186169107 :answer-field/choices 17592186169109] [:db/retract 17592186169107 :answer-field/choices 17592186169110] [:db/retract 17592186169107 :answer-field/choices 17592186169111] [:db/retract 17592186169107 :answer-field/choices 17592186169112] [:db/retract 17592186169101 :answer-field/choices 17592186169104] [:db/retract 17592186169101 :answer-field/choices 17592186169105] [:db/retract 17592186169101 :answer-field/choices 17592186169106] [:db/retract 17592186169101 :answer-field/choices 17592186169102] [:db/retract 17592186169101 :answer-field/choices 17592186169103] {:db/id 17592186172105 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d6ff97d1-caaf-54b3-95a7-b3c5d233fee4" "question-916f2a38-b006-5a12-8bd4-a4cea4898a65" "question-157efc5d-8a57-58a8-ada2-5fc55f4e29c9" "question-55964901-1ac8-5dce-b560-5abe87c95ae4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171683 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n_1=29$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma_1=7$ and a sample of size $n_2=23$ from a normal population with standard deviation $\\sigma_2=9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are $\\overline{x}_1=15$ and  $\\overline{x}_2=9,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*\n\n![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are the population variances,  $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 15-9 = 6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.96) = 0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.025} = 1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE = 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{7^2}{29}+\\dfrac{9^2}{23}} \\approx 4.474\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 6\\pm 4.474\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172106 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ce81f23c-d71c-5733-bd29-cfc419d8f4e9" "question-5a88f00c-6713-5649-aec8-434c7227c5ed" "question-a800aa72-0f52-5352-92d7-7cc58c7677c8" "question-c2323ee0-90b5-5053-934c-27a812c3f1ca"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171684 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size $n_1=68$ from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma_1=6$ and a sample of size $n_2=35$ from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma_2=5.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are $\\overline{x}_1=18$ and  $\\overline{x}_2=15,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05,$ where $Z \\sim N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\geq30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_1-\\mu_2$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2) \\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E=z_{\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_1^2}{n_1}+\\dfrac{\\sigma_2^2}{n_2}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- $\\overline{x}_1$ and $\\overline{x}_2$ are the sample means, $\\mu_1$ and $\\mu_2$ are the population means, $\\sigma_1^2$ and $\\sigma_2^2$ are the population variances,  $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}_1-\\overline{x}_2 = 18-15 = 3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.9=0.1 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z > 1.645) = 0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\nz_{\\alpha/2} = z_{0.05} = 1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE = 1.645 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{6^2}{68}+\\dfrac{5^2}{35}} \\approx 1.835\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3\\pm 1.835\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172129 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e0803d61-1e04-581c-a011-ddebaf5fd353" "question-b65f05d5-c649-50dc-a60f-542de8b1eeb2" "question-394c3561-19e2-5323-928c-73d8d3b37d89"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186171709 :question/problem "The diameters of balls for bearings produced by machines ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) have standard deviations of ![](images/d1/d119f2a860c3a45855fddc0be7b90afe3ef3b9c9a1d9e12f6f292887360e797d.svg) and ![](images/47/47d7f608a187818bc771a90371ec6ed6fd92a8446815fb911253e4f545627c81.svg) respectively. Engineers want to know whether the population mean ![](images/61/61959f998d5bf057f78b23b849c800b479b5640bee3f604626eb721872cffc20.svg) for the diameter of balls produced by machine ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) is larger than the population mean ![](images/55/550e73c341b931075845607f5f4bcece014f3088cfb1148197b696933d13545a.svg) for the diameter of balls produced by machine ![](images/02/029615e92d8b6d5c444a4daf74a9a8d63048e8d4be5f97c8207fe7af2e189e18.svg) They construct a ![](images/4a/4aa35f22590ee68ad0ff0b07b01c41e9caf9e2ad6c73a305f297e918d684e045.svg) confidence interval for the difference between the population means.   \n\nGiven that the engineers sampled ![](images/8c/8c0d9d65f3495da5c81dd0e397e9c32f09f2440810847b109055a8cdf9b63c1d.svg) balls produced by machine ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/45/4554df746f5a593617a7882989a71995d6e7a6be8ef47225843dafb9e8d746e3.svg) balls made by machine ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) and found that their mean diameters are ![](images/3a/3ac1972c8c6e03b02a5569c5ccf4f0cd79744fc783050367109d053c72c56e2e.svg) and ![](images/b3/b3294c6196c0bb447f4261b566f3f910eff97c9591565bc2b35ac7798c3a5e97.svg) respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the ![](images/4a/4aa35f22590ee68ad0ff0b07b01c41e9caf9e2ad6c73a305f297e918d684e045.svg) confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately ![](images/be/be02ea5f949b002400b734a0b683d09930c91c625e8be7d7a44a4eae822476c9.svg)\n\nII. All values contained within the ![](images/4a/4aa35f22590ee68ad0ff0b07b01c41e9caf9e2ad6c73a305f297e918d684e045.svg) confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ![](images/00/00d6a9d8ff1bdcb7095099ba2d0b2192b4df6bf4328ee093825ec36db7191df5.svg)\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable ![](images/bf/bf4994a6e13924c5bc1f82242e6b12a6e452ebdb5c9f607b35c2614dab4ba30e.svg) the table below shows the ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)-scores ![](images/f9/f948084d308bf70eed90f1ddc186f4d4b6ffd5b03b4a84db77741ca7acb0697c.svg) such that ![](images/2e/2e84a088a2421fdf8a026b804a2a39d41831915f78d6ca5abcf7c95644ea3f98.svg) for some particular values of ![](images/1d/1d3e45623fb292952d8c207690797505cc7c484a6410f4a95aff94a51b4a0ee5.svg)*\n\n| ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) | ![](images/14/14eb5f107102d64032e5c62207a5f22c7080993cccdec49809d85677e32228dc.svg) | ![](images/f4/f40b54dcb945a64594b78fd581e4f950c4d9c07d15f30d9f1dc019f46c02cdde.svg) | ![](images/11/1156c0c032864aee44a0c788d626a41dc2f342323fa089a19d82027b0ae5a2e4.svg) | ![](images/96/96bea0a68c23edf8d74b7757824baa031b2b7d0bbabd8a9babe5b9bf72a0a13e.svg) | ![](images/bf/bf7be53403f336e34ad868c68d24905fb2eafba87ae7c65ebfa0796fd137efe0.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/f9/f948084d308bf70eed90f1ddc186f4d4b6ffd5b03b4a84db77741ca7acb0697c.svg) | ![](images/12/12781236356b8a089115083f90b012544e848846f64395d4b9e0a5f2ca689c93.svg) | ![](images/b5/b5f9496f7a1bda32cf8f921e43a5bb42c820b118ebe241131cd7a38b869061b7.svg) | ![](images/0e/0e11b9a3e1e1a424a8db948ce46afebd8abc9faa855574ea12b8760c1da2ebf9.svg) | ![](images/18/185c18f81476cc288ddc92a0105a258c4b0e89893c75c7df3a6b366d6cdcf9ee.svg) | ![](images/13/1345290aa983a990a0e9592b1156be4bd26490599086e86ec7ccf83313f1412e.svg) |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., ![](images/7e/7e953b8222eba132ef65fbeead25790198f988c2e0cd5625aa5de0fda0fefa1a.svg) and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value ![](images/79/7995b2daef1650484d2fa6518cb775356ae12ecdf597eba9e2e04f4f35dee93c.svg) between ![](images/15/156b31ede73de5301befe67f02745a0202f1806a62f9fdecc6fc9ee3f21117ba.svg) and ![](images/76/76543f4805f22715457d55feb2a3c87d0d873bdcb9c1eed7a4f68539269dcf59.svg) the corresponding ![](images/1f/1fb6279301c8fe6dce4a6f502a17e6e4abeaa62a44a9ab5023a1d3ccf4aac5c4.svg) confidence interval for the difference ![](images/9d/9d6dac96cd69f7064940eae6e63d88c95f1fc4a92b102ba3693dd819ac128126.svg) between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n![](images/5d/5d808254159d48a94531e110e5d9c4c8fabc2f800c5615802abccfa7bc8ea622.svg)\n\nwhere \n\n- ![](images/4b/4b7b988b2e9ef88f674ce655d7d2f15af2f4e9172bd7a9d6cce2fe0aa72e82df.svg) is the margin of error,\n\n- ![](images/69/6937033abbb07640e7bfa8def543b2a045bb5e1775a939ab76a5fcca1cb0a0ea.svg) and ![](images/fc/fca756bcf0457e144eb84a5bfd67bb3f4432d22de2e7d0f3c5fb2e9c78727da9.svg) are the sample means, ![](images/6d/6d3486d14fef4cbd7f15af9097617bd5e675baa1f13cb86f69f09bef24ee4829.svg) and ![](images/a2/a21f07d3948532bb6aa91995da9b6d602ad4b8628d9ea967ad3f3d135f9ba533.svg) are the population means, ![](images/20/20f6be5e75cac9315a72adf7baab3c361dbb69fe57709526e03c9a023af587af.svg) and ![](images/65/65622d587fccd0fe592e6a5dce2ed965999dd2e90a288087469f8c4c70eb3503.svg) are the population variances,  ![](images/de/de44911177478b672559d6311b30df7c906693cd480fe64d8dcf78c08412d61e.svg) and ![](images/07/072df845480f1dd134dc71854a3d9aeacde7563aaca739d5c718f5068d5ee300.svg) are the sample sizes,\n\n- ![](images/af/af38b9349332c35a9833820ab5fa0f3540b7655405c8b2ca17587f3db84f53bf.svg) and ![](images/c1/c1a5b6740351318fb48420ebbed2f09086cd253fb88b802c8f6bf138640d75fa.svg)\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n![](images/c1/c1d47fdc3028b6d69a77ad99d91ad6031d852da3aad276e0ff8e792b12edecc3.svg)\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a ![](images/4a/4aa35f22590ee68ad0ff0b07b01c41e9caf9e2ad6c73a305f297e918d684e045.svg) confidence interval, we have \n![](images/bb/bb227d4b2458830d95e344377c1633020fb3ce3bc3f27acfb7ec28f3473c6548.svg)\nWe also need to find the ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)-score value ![](images/ff/ff6062321d513483e6075fddac7ccaa5d848044aa39968f15e2e120e23e09cc4.svg) such that ![](images/98/98124d1c0a053f7a1651a849ef0f233f42ca9c8bae4154aaed3bd995b88dcdb7.svg) where ![](images/c1/c1a5b6740351318fb48420ebbed2f09086cd253fb88b802c8f6bf138640d75fa.svg)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ![](images/5b/5b048df55a97617e12801c6e121edf26dfc90b10795b7fb3c3e57990511bc3ca.svg) \n\n| ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) | ![](images/14/14eb5f107102d64032e5c62207a5f22c7080993cccdec49809d85677e32228dc.svg) | ![](images/f4/f40b54dcb945a64594b78fd581e4f950c4d9c07d15f30d9f1dc019f46c02cdde.svg) | ![](images/11/1156c0c032864aee44a0c788d626a41dc2f342323fa089a19d82027b0ae5a2e4.svg) | ![](images/96/96bea0a68c23edf8d74b7757824baa031b2b7d0bbabd8a9babe5b9bf72a0a13e.svg) | ![](images/c9/c9b3bdfe48f79c3efdc3f67699556323b7f2df004dc4e9957e2e2fd18a98494c.svg) |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ![](images/f9/f948084d308bf70eed90f1ddc186f4d4b6ffd5b03b4a84db77741ca7acb0697c.svg) | ![](images/12/12781236356b8a089115083f90b012544e848846f64395d4b9e0a5f2ca689c93.svg) | ![](images/b5/b5f9496f7a1bda32cf8f921e43a5bb42c820b118ebe241131cd7a38b869061b7.svg) | ![](images/0e/0e11b9a3e1e1a424a8db948ce46afebd8abc9faa855574ea12b8760c1da2ebf9.svg) | ![](images/18/185c18f81476cc288ddc92a0105a258c4b0e89893c75c7df3a6b366d6cdcf9ee.svg) | ![](images/15/15b83a8ca53afabe815ab94d820effad249c1e7b05bb06c20bf94badc051a9da.svg) |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n![](images/4a/4a3f5d764d317efe5be018c3be111afdb40da1316dc74fe9ccf8037274260ee7.svg) \n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n![](images/0c/0c73cf36767ddad8a08ec9fe256a146af7d251921180cdb7397c69a5a2fa6baf.svg)\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are ![](images/be/be02ea5f949b002400b734a0b683d09930c91c625e8be7d7a44a4eae822476c9.svg)\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains zero.\n![](images/a0/a08641be702b83463c7c420e7ae8f77d69564f4c1734f3d809efe7a4027fed7e.svg)\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ![](images/52/52ee5d6a0009f029e4a9917fbff9deecf964de59a9bea34c3461695582c597ba.svg)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d6ff97d1-caaf-54b3-95a7-b3c5d233fee4" :question/id #uuid "d6ff97d1-caaf-54b3-95a7-b3c5d233fee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159651" :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=25$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=21$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=9.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=15$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=16,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=15-16=-1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{10}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{9}^{2}}{21}}≈7.221\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -1\\pm 7.221\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b17dccba-23c2-5ab8-b49f-4b01afd89316"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b17dccba-23c2-5ab8-b49f-4b01afd89316" :answer-field/id #uuid "b17dccba-23c2-5ab8-b49f-4b01afd89316" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a0e4826f-6da2-57e3-8a4c-9428bfbbc0ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a0e4826f-6da2-57e3-8a4c-9428bfbbc0ef" "answer-60d30b43-7393-5a6d-8a00-faffee94d6df" "answer-84a1ad66-15d0-56cc-8056-5f7dce60061c" "answer-d6e3437f-42a6-599c-a6f6-285f76c3e2d6" "answer-b3ae43f2-6b8a-5de1-a2b1-e342b57223eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0e4826f-6da2-57e3-8a4c-9428bfbbc0ef" :answer/id #uuid "a0e4826f-6da2-57e3-8a4c-9428bfbbc0ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\pm 7.221" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-60d30b43-7393-5a6d-8a00-faffee94d6df" :answer/id #uuid "60d30b43-7393-5a6d-8a00-faffee94d6df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\pm 7.435" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84a1ad66-15d0-56cc-8056-5f7dce60061c" :answer/id #uuid "84a1ad66-15d0-56cc-8056-5f7dce60061c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1\\pm 7.683" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6e3437f-42a6-599c-a6f6-285f76c3e2d6" :answer/id #uuid "d6e3437f-42a6-599c-a6f6-285f76c3e2d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pm 6.679" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3ae43f2-6b8a-5de1-a2b1-e342b57223eb" :answer/id #uuid "b3ae43f2-6b8a-5de1-a2b1-e342b57223eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pm 6.846" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-916f2a38-b006-5a12-8bd4-a4cea4898a65" :question/id #uuid "916f2a38-b006-5a12-8bd4-a4cea4898a65" :question/math-academy-id "q-159650" :question/problem "![](images/64/6486d3914bb0f7b4898220512e96630d13d2b0f4a5c1851938f98371a4c0255f.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=26$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=9$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=21$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=8.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=17$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=20,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=17-20=-3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{9}^{2}}{26}+\\frac{{8}^{2}}{21}}≈6.395\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3\\pm 6.395\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1eb71659-be46-5935-ba67-718a939af1f2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1eb71659-be46-5935-ba67-718a939af1f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "1eb71659-be46-5935-ba67-718a939af1f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-78efb13f-9673-5a6d-9a43-2da8b02e2061" :answer-field/choices ["answer-78efb13f-9673-5a6d-9a43-2da8b02e2061" "answer-eb5457ab-70ab-5540-acf8-02a050045282" "answer-a46fffa5-c8aa-536d-bf41-d98cefa06600" "answer-c6c0effa-857f-52b0-8b89-9e48f3ef3215" "answer-bb498ffd-75c3-5362-8360-9312a52ca3f8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78efb13f-9673-5a6d-9a43-2da8b02e2061" :answer/id #uuid "78efb13f-9673-5a6d-9a43-2da8b02e2061" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\pm 6.395" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb5457ab-70ab-5540-acf8-02a050045282" :answer/id #uuid "eb5457ab-70ab-5540-acf8-02a050045282" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\pm 6.489" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a46fffa5-c8aa-536d-bf41-d98cefa06600" :answer/id #uuid "a46fffa5-c8aa-536d-bf41-d98cefa06600" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\pm 6.589" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6c0effa-857f-52b0-8b89-9e48f3ef3215" :answer/id #uuid "c6c0effa-857f-52b0-8b89-9e48f3ef3215" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\pm 5.789" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb498ffd-75c3-5362-8360-9312a52ca3f8" :answer/id #uuid "bb498ffd-75c3-5362-8360-9312a52ca3f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\pm 5.873" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-157efc5d-8a57-58a8-ada2-5fc55f4e29c9" :question/id #uuid "157efc5d-8a57-58a8-ada2-5fc55f4e29c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159646" :question/problem "![](images/69/693576483310a4656435ea4a3d8d9652b8de563d26a87c7c80fb902a3bef1e29.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=14$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=3$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=16$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=9$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=7,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=9-7=2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{3}^{2}}{14}+\\frac{{5}^{2}}{16}}≈2.911\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 2\\pm 2.911\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da96cd01-655c-5aad-b1ac-38d2723dadfa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-da96cd01-655c-5aad-b1ac-38d2723dadfa" :answer-field/id #uuid "da96cd01-655c-5aad-b1ac-38d2723dadfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bf1bcfde-923f-5031-a349-fb1978beefc5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4b82ae34-fc82-5522-95ce-288cdfc60c14" "answer-bf1bcfde-923f-5031-a349-fb1978beefc5" "answer-9813fb09-9a2d-5dcc-a465-e9389bfcf2bf" "answer-4973b038-5f39-5534-aee7-34713e78281b" "answer-e1e7cc53-3921-5765-9657-d7965a1dd19c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b82ae34-fc82-5522-95ce-288cdfc60c14" :answer/id #uuid "4b82ae34-fc82-5522-95ce-288cdfc60c14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm 2.617" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf1bcfde-923f-5031-a349-fb1978beefc5" :answer/id #uuid "bf1bcfde-923f-5031-a349-fb1978beefc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm 2.911" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9813fb09-9a2d-5dcc-a465-e9389bfcf2bf" :answer/id #uuid "9813fb09-9a2d-5dcc-a465-e9389bfcf2bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pm 3.109" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4973b038-5f39-5534-aee7-34713e78281b" :answer/id #uuid "4973b038-5f39-5534-aee7-34713e78281b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pm 3.184" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1e7cc53-3921-5765-9657-d7965a1dd19c" :answer/id #uuid "e1e7cc53-3921-5765-9657-d7965a1dd19c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pm 3.456" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172374 :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=23$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=28$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=7.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=21$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=25,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=21-25=-4\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{23}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{28}}≈2.771\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -4\\pm 2.771\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186169107 :answer-field/correct "answer-d9e07a6a-8240-5544-96c1-be08f41d01a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-283a3441-c0c0-5d33-b1e2-95d020430232" "answer-c01c198b-0134-5374-bf17-62d16cb81133" "answer-9dbb7478-fadc-55a0-8cdc-5d1784873613" "answer-d9e07a6a-8240-5544-96c1-be08f41d01a9" "answer-dce3af0e-2ba7-5bdc-aa32-0401e94fd7d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-283a3441-c0c0-5d33-b1e2-95d020430232" :answer/id #uuid "283a3441-c0c0-5d33-b1e2-95d020430232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\pm 2.687" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c01c198b-0134-5374-bf17-62d16cb81133" :answer/id #uuid "c01c198b-0134-5374-bf17-62d16cb81133" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3\\pm 2.879" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9dbb7478-fadc-55a0-8cdc-5d1784873613" :answer/id #uuid "9dbb7478-fadc-55a0-8cdc-5d1784873613" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\pm 2.619" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9e07a6a-8240-5544-96c1-be08f41d01a9" :answer/id #uuid "d9e07a6a-8240-5544-96c1-be08f41d01a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\pm 2.771" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dce3af0e-2ba7-5bdc-aa32-0401e94fd7d4" :answer/id #uuid "dce3af0e-2ba7-5bdc-aa32-0401e94fd7d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\pm 2.817" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-55964901-1ac8-5dce-b560-5abe87c95ae4" :question/id #uuid "55964901-1ac8-5dce-b560-5abe87c95ae4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159649" :question/problem "![](images/39/39a83b1081d22919c9549c4ff1c6ad8006dc802cc23f9802d762e97883bea48c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=17$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=6$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=23$ from a normal population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=6$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=12,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=6-12=-6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{6}^{2}}{17}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{23}}≈2.606\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -6\\pm 2.606\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-78afa67e-d9b8-56b9-b313-ff0bb8f6f118"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-78afa67e-d9b8-56b9-b313-ff0bb8f6f118" :answer-field/id #uuid "78afa67e-d9b8-56b9-b313-ff0bb8f6f118" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bb64f93f-9e2f-511c-a48a-9bf774a699c0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-47fed120-1d44-5ea5-a77d-e3c2e40cdf28" "answer-fa18b279-af23-595c-b41f-d1624f2de0d8" "answer-b7ec9ecf-c932-5246-8583-70554e17a596" "answer-df47b00f-4283-5d5f-9724-575f868a3802" "answer-bb64f93f-9e2f-511c-a48a-9bf774a699c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47fed120-1d44-5ea5-a77d-e3c2e40cdf28" :answer/id #uuid "47fed120-1d44-5ea5-a77d-e3c2e40cdf28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\pm 1.798" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa18b279-af23-595c-b41f-d1624f2de0d8" :answer/id #uuid "fa18b279-af23-595c-b41f-d1624f2de0d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5\\pm 1.968" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b7ec9ecf-c932-5246-8583-70554e17a596" :answer/id #uuid "b7ec9ecf-c932-5246-8583-70554e17a596" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\pm 2.229" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df47b00f-4283-5d5f-9724-575f868a3802" :answer/id #uuid "df47b00f-4283-5d5f-9724-575f868a3802" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\pm 2.438" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb64f93f-9e2f-511c-a48a-9bf774a699c0" :answer/id #uuid "bb64f93f-9e2f-511c-a48a-9bf774a699c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\pm 2.606" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ce81f23c-d71c-5733-bd29-cfc419d8f4e9" :question/id #uuid "ce81f23c-d71c-5733-bd29-cfc419d8f4e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159631" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=98$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=63$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=31$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=37,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=31-37=-6\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{98}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{63}}≈1.038\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -6\\pm 1.038\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-589dd3aa-27ed-55b2-b164-358bcc396c7c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-589dd3aa-27ed-55b2-b164-358bcc396c7c" :answer-field/id #uuid "589dd3aa-27ed-55b2-b164-358bcc396c7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-229c5f18-9463-50d8-822d-ccc6b223d2b4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-229c5f18-9463-50d8-822d-ccc6b223d2b4" "answer-ecac826c-81d2-50de-9062-f66867e90ca7" "answer-0f0f6794-b4ff-5127-a887-5ccf8da54017" "answer-bd94bfad-cadb-5ab7-a2fb-2585eb0f5a6d" "answer-1ef338df-0f9e-5b97-8308-552bcb3b86cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-229c5f18-9463-50d8-822d-ccc6b223d2b4" :answer/id #uuid "229c5f18-9463-50d8-822d-ccc6b223d2b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\pm 1.038" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ecac826c-81d2-50de-9062-f66867e90ca7" :answer/id #uuid "ecac826c-81d2-50de-9062-f66867e90ca7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\pm 1.453" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f0f6794-b4ff-5127-a887-5ccf8da54017" :answer/id #uuid "0f0f6794-b4ff-5127-a887-5ccf8da54017" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\pm 1.517" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd94bfad-cadb-5ab7-a2fb-2585eb0f5a6d" :answer/id #uuid "bd94bfad-cadb-5ab7-a2fb-2585eb0f5a6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\pm 1.655" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1ef338df-0f9e-5b97-8308-552bcb3b86cb" :answer/id #uuid "1ef338df-0f9e-5b97-8308-552bcb3b86cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6\\pm 1.833" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5a88f00c-6713-5649-aec8-434c7227c5ed" :question/id #uuid "5a88f00c-6713-5649-aec8-434c7227c5ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-159629" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=62$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=2$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=37$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.1.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=5$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=2,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=5-2=3\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{62}+\\frac{{3.1}^{2}}{37}}≈0.937\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3\\pm 0.937\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-13f570ad-c43b-57e8-ba93-0fc1150a2f90"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-13f570ad-c43b-57e8-ba93-0fc1150a2f90" :answer-field/id #uuid "13f570ad-c43b-57e8-ba93-0fc1150a2f90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c6928ebd-d517-53d2-9d99-868dd521cf49" :answer-field/choices ["answer-926dfafc-ce4a-5046-951b-9ccf381dc6bd" "answer-79750ee5-faa4-5ac2-81c0-5577db5d5bd9" "answer-c6928ebd-d517-53d2-9d99-868dd521cf49" "answer-a9bcad9a-b7e1-5665-9bbe-0e18f5490c65" "answer-5659876a-67ff-5346-a481-846ce012b452"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-926dfafc-ce4a-5046-951b-9ccf381dc6bd" :answer/id #uuid "926dfafc-ce4a-5046-951b-9ccf381dc6bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pm 0.533" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79750ee5-faa4-5ac2-81c0-5577db5d5bd9" :answer/id #uuid "79750ee5-faa4-5ac2-81c0-5577db5d5bd9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pm 0.560" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6928ebd-d517-53d2-9d99-868dd521cf49" :answer/id #uuid "c6928ebd-d517-53d2-9d99-868dd521cf49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pm 0.937" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9bcad9a-b7e1-5665-9bbe-0e18f5490c65" :answer/id #uuid "a9bcad9a-b7e1-5665-9bbe-0e18f5490c65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pm 1.232" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5659876a-67ff-5346-a481-846ce012b452" :answer/id #uuid "5659876a-67ff-5346-a481-846ce012b452" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\pm 1.256" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a800aa72-0f52-5352-92d7-7cc58c7677c8" :question/id #uuid "a800aa72-0f52-5352-92d7-7cc58c7677c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159633" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=128$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.2$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=49$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=53$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=52,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=53-52=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7.2}^{2}}{128}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{49}}≈1.676\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 1.676\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7bb68ec-1f3f-5057-8cda-d0404350a2d7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7bb68ec-1f3f-5057-8cda-d0404350a2d7" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7bb68ec-1f3f-5057-8cda-d0404350a2d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0409437b-a2c5-53b2-928b-acf3d1eb2629" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c70e8092-9342-5f79-92c0-102cf831a41b" "answer-f64f8075-7077-5fb5-8136-62fa215db108" "answer-65526a15-276a-5615-b04f-62469c607d50" "answer-0409437b-a2c5-53b2-928b-acf3d1eb2629" "answer-8e8c0ca1-5962-5403-b6b1-c4dbeb55c5e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c70e8092-9342-5f79-92c0-102cf831a41b" :answer/id #uuid "c70e8092-9342-5f79-92c0-102cf831a41b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\pm 0.728" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f64f8075-7077-5fb5-8136-62fa215db108" :answer/id #uuid "f64f8075-7077-5fb5-8136-62fa215db108" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\pm 1.434" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65526a15-276a-5615-b04f-62469c607d50" :answer/id #uuid "65526a15-276a-5615-b04f-62469c607d50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\pm 1.637" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0409437b-a2c5-53b2-928b-acf3d1eb2629" :answer/id #uuid "0409437b-a2c5-53b2-928b-acf3d1eb2629" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\pm 1.676" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e8c0ca1-5962-5403-b6b1-c4dbeb55c5e3" :answer/id #uuid "8e8c0ca1-5962-5403-b6b1-c4dbeb55c5e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1\\pm 2.367" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c2323ee0-90b5-5053-934c-27a812c3f1ca" :question/id #uuid "c2323ee0-90b5-5053-934c-27a812c3f1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-159627" :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=46$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=67$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.2.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=17$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=31,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=17-31=-14\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.96\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4}^{2}}{46}+\\frac{{3.2}^{2}}{67}}≈1.387\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -14\\pm 1.387\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-036316b9-78c6-568f-97e6-f24937589101"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-036316b9-78c6-568f-97e6-f24937589101" :answer-field/id #uuid "036316b9-78c6-568f-97e6-f24937589101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e1ccb036-87c7-57c1-b7c2-4bc6a0ecf5eb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a382ec2a-1be0-55d9-8223-ffcaf3323116" "answer-1099919c-3ad8-52a0-bf85-4786225bb35b" "answer-e1ccb036-87c7-57c1-b7c2-4bc6a0ecf5eb" "answer-5977604f-0c53-576d-9bf5-ffb432edcde9" "answer-107fe77d-b432-5e5d-a714-2ad39721738a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a382ec2a-1be0-55d9-8223-ffcaf3323116" :answer/id #uuid "a382ec2a-1be0-55d9-8223-ffcaf3323116" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14\\pm 0.719" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1099919c-3ad8-52a0-bf85-4786225bb35b" :answer/id #uuid "1099919c-3ad8-52a0-bf85-4786225bb35b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14\\pm 0.981" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1ccb036-87c7-57c1-b7c2-4bc6a0ecf5eb" :answer/id #uuid "e1ccb036-87c7-57c1-b7c2-4bc6a0ecf5eb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14\\pm 1.387" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5977604f-0c53-576d-9bf5-ffb432edcde9" :answer/id #uuid "5977604f-0c53-576d-9bf5-ffb432edcde9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14\\pm 1.922" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-107fe77d-b432-5e5d-a714-2ad39721738a" :answer/id #uuid "107fe77d-b432-5e5d-a714-2ad39721738a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-14\\pm 1.96" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172373 :question/problem "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=57$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=94$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=22$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=29,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=22-29=-7\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>2.576)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.005}=2.576.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{57}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{94}}≈2.518\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -7\\pm 2.518\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186169101 :answer-field/correct "answer-1d63990b-f2f6-55fc-b457-f831826e44c5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a0db282-85d1-5448-ab0f-a0a104792929" "answer-085068f5-8a74-5a8c-ab35-10473d7d13ba" "answer-1d63990b-f2f6-55fc-b457-f831826e44c5" "answer-92d0f8f0-eb37-55f9-9982-76818ef21ecf" "answer-7bb68cf4-10fe-55ad-84c2-8e802a1021ee"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a0db282-85d1-5448-ab0f-a0a104792929" :answer/id #uuid "6a0db282-85d1-5448-ab0f-a0a104792929" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\pm 1.013" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-085068f5-8a74-5a8c-ab35-10473d7d13ba" :answer/id #uuid "085068f5-8a74-5a8c-ab35-10473d7d13ba" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\pm 2.461" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d63990b-f2f6-55fc-b457-f831826e44c5" :answer/id #uuid "1d63990b-f2f6-55fc-b457-f831826e44c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\pm 2.518" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92d0f8f0-eb37-55f9-9982-76818ef21ecf" :answer/id #uuid "92d0f8f0-eb37-55f9-9982-76818ef21ecf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\pm 2.576" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7bb68cf4-10fe-55ad-84c2-8e802a1021ee" :answer/id #uuid "7bb68cf4-10fe-55ad-84c2-8e802a1021ee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7\\pm 3.185" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e0803d61-1e04-581c-a011-ddebaf5fd353" :question/id #uuid "e0803d61-1e04-581c-a011-ddebaf5fd353" :question/math-academy-id "q-159644" :question/problem "It is known that sales (quantities) of a new product in two different states X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=7$, respectively. A marketing agency wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of sales in state X is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of sales in state Y. They decide to construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the two population means.\n\nThe agency sampled $14$ shops from state X and $15$ shops from state Y, finding that their mean sales were $54.5$ and $48.6$, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $5.9\\pm 7.630.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=54.5-48.6=5.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n \n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{14}+\\frac{{7}^{2}}{15}}≈5.790\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 5.9\\pm 5.790\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $5.9\\pm 5.790.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are positive:\n\n$$\n(5.9-5.790,\\,5.9+5.790)=(0.11,\\,11.69)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are positive, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ede57a7a-8e37-5d0b-866a-4f7edf2e1203"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ede57a7a-8e37-5d0b-866a-4f7edf2e1203" :answer-field/id #uuid "ede57a7a-8e37-5d0b-866a-4f7edf2e1203" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dc5f7146-ae17-5c2f-89c8-94d3090bce16" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a8bbfc0-e7fe-52a8-ae44-1d26fdf713f0" "answer-7d81a17f-36f8-5f95-9e05-bf828e988da1" "answer-dc5f7146-ae17-5c2f-89c8-94d3090bce16" "answer-801293a6-a6ee-5582-abdd-6c2eac5cec4c" "answer-2aeb8b8c-08de-50a8-a2b4-a4dac8256ee5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a8bbfc0-e7fe-52a8-ae44-1d26fdf713f0" :answer/id #uuid "1a8bbfc0-e7fe-52a8-ae44-1d26fdf713f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d81a17f-36f8-5f95-9e05-bf828e988da1" :answer/id #uuid "7d81a17f-36f8-5f95-9e05-bf828e988da1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc5f7146-ae17-5c2f-89c8-94d3090bce16" :answer/id #uuid "dc5f7146-ae17-5c2f-89c8-94d3090bce16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-801293a6-a6ee-5582-abdd-6c2eac5cec4c" :answer/id #uuid "801293a6-a6ee-5582-abdd-6c2eac5cec4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2aeb8b8c-08de-50a8-a2b4-a4dac8256ee5" :answer/id #uuid "2aeb8b8c-08de-50a8-a2b4-a4dac8256ee5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172369 :question/problem "The scores on the final calculus test in states X and Y are known to be normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=5$ points and ${\\sigma }_{y}=4$ points, respectively. Scientists want to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of scores in state X is smaller than the population mean of scores in state Y. They decide to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe scientists sampled $35$ students from state X and $23$ students from state Y, finding that their mean scores are $85$ points and $87$ points, respectively. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-2\\pm 2.105.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=85-87=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{5}^{2}}{35}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{23}}≈1.953\n$$\n\n \n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -2\\pm 1.953\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-2\\pm 1.953.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are negative:\n\n$$\n(-2-1.953,\\,-2+1.953)=(-3.953,\\,-0.047)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are negative, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186169077 :answer-field/correct 17592186169082 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172370 :question/problem "Previous studies show that the scores of girls and boys on a particular test for dyslexia have standard deviations of $2$ points and $4$ points, respectively. Scientists want to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ for girls is the same as the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ for boys. They construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nScientists sampled $125$ girls and $118$ boys and found that their mean scores on the test were $42$ points and $41$ points. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $1\\pm 1.055.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=42-41=1\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{2}^{2}}{125}+\\frac{{4}^{2}}{118}}≈1.055\n$$\n\n \n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 1\\pm 1.055\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $1\\pm 1.055.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$\n\n$$\n(1-1.055,\\,1+1.055)=(-0.055,\\,2.055)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186169083 :answer-field/correct 17592186169085 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b65f05d5-c649-50dc-a60f-542de8b1eeb2" :question/id #uuid "b65f05d5-c649-50dc-a60f-542de8b1eeb2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159625" :question/problem "The weights of two age groups of men, X and Y, are normally distributed with a standard deviation of  ${\\sigma }_{x}=10\\,\\text{kg},$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=12\\,\\text{kg},$ respectively.\nA researcher wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the men's weights in group X is less than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the men's weights in group Y. He decides to construct a $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe researcher sampled $28$ men from group X and $30$ men from group Y, finding that their mean weights are $74\\,kg$ and $82\\,kg,$ respectively. Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The end-points of the $99%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-8\\pm 4.005.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $99%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=74-82=-8\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.005}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.005})=0.005,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.005}=2.576:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=2.576\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{10}^{2}}{28}+\\frac{{12}^{2}}{30}}≈7.453\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -8\\pm 7.453\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-8\\pm 7.453.$\n\n- Statement II is true. All values within the confidence interval are negative:\n\n$$\n(-8-7.453,\\,-8+7.453)=(-15.453,\\,-0.547)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since all values within the confidence interval are negative, this suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8efbc2d7-abce-5db8-9ce0-ff02e6978bfc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8efbc2d7-abce-5db8-9ce0-ff02e6978bfc" :answer-field/id #uuid "8efbc2d7-abce-5db8-9ce0-ff02e6978bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-469786b7-4252-564e-a019-cd79a0addf4f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-19465a71-2c42-5c5b-bf95-b79015398ab2" "answer-1d59393f-1c83-55c1-a14b-2582c48643bc" "answer-469786b7-4252-564e-a019-cd79a0addf4f" "answer-201cc3b4-0358-5baa-8b26-c3707f290a81" "answer-5fb0b782-7d29-5719-901e-f1c7ad53889c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19465a71-2c42-5c5b-bf95-b79015398ab2" :answer/id #uuid "19465a71-2c42-5c5b-bf95-b79015398ab2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d59393f-1c83-55c1-a14b-2582c48643bc" :answer/id #uuid "1d59393f-1c83-55c1-a14b-2582c48643bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-469786b7-4252-564e-a019-cd79a0addf4f" :answer/id #uuid "469786b7-4252-564e-a019-cd79a0addf4f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-201cc3b4-0358-5baa-8b26-c3707f290a81" :answer/id #uuid "201cc3b4-0358-5baa-8b26-c3707f290a81" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5fb0b782-7d29-5719-901e-f1c7ad53889c" :answer/id #uuid "5fb0b782-7d29-5719-901e-f1c7ad53889c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-394c3561-19e2-5323-928c-73d8d3b37d89" :question/id #uuid "394c3561-19e2-5323-928c-73d8d3b37d89" :question/math-academy-id "q-159639" :question/problem "It is known that the weights of oranges from farms X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=7$ grams and ${\\sigma }_{y}=8$ grams, respectively. The owner of the farms wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the weights of oranges from farm X is smaller than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the weights of oranges from farm Y. He decides to construct a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nThe farmer sampled $25$ oranges from farm X and $20$ oranges from farm Y, finding that their mean weights are $125$ grams and $128.2$ grams, respectively. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $-3.2\\pm 3.737.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $90%$ confidence interval are negative.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}<{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both populations are normally distributed, and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=125-128.2=-3.2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $z$-score value ${z}_{0.05}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{0.05})=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$\nFrom the percentage points table of the normal distribution, we obtain that ${z}_{0.05}=1.645:$ \n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{7}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{8}^{2}}{20}}≈3.737\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & -3.2\\pm 3.737\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. The end-points of our confidence interval are $-3.2\\pm 3.737.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The confidence interval contains $0:$ \n\n$$\n(-3.2-3.737,\\,-3.2+3.737)=(-6.937,\\,0.537)\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the confidence interval contains zero, it remains possible that ${\\mu }_{x}={\\mu }_{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9fb8f9ee-2d32-5724-b54f-4283d651b51b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9fb8f9ee-2d32-5724-b54f-4283d651b51b" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fb8f9ee-2d32-5724-b54f-4283d651b51b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0e8bfbf9-a916-5b30-9995-bcb268797646" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f01f181e-82f2-5865-9bc5-a4b20ee3a50d" "answer-0e8bfbf9-a916-5b30-9995-bcb268797646" "answer-fbe0d9ad-5199-54ff-8dac-b93c7a287ead" "answer-dad6bc31-1c34-52a6-9bef-8f2fa704739b" "answer-1eff722a-3a3d-57c9-96db-e3afde6bd3f5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f01f181e-82f2-5865-9bc5-a4b20ee3a50d" :answer/id #uuid "f01f181e-82f2-5865-9bc5-a4b20ee3a50d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e8bfbf9-a916-5b30-9995-bcb268797646" :answer/id #uuid "0e8bfbf9-a916-5b30-9995-bcb268797646" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbe0d9ad-5199-54ff-8dac-b93c7a287ead" :answer/id #uuid "fbe0d9ad-5199-54ff-8dac-b93c7a287ead" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dad6bc31-1c34-52a6-9bef-8f2fa704739b" :answer/id #uuid "dad6bc31-1c34-52a6-9bef-8f2fa704739b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1eff722a-3a3d-57c9-96db-e3afde6bd3f5" :answer/id #uuid "1eff722a-3a3d-57c9-96db-e3afde6bd3f5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173250 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to construct confidence intervals for the difference between two population means in cases where the population variances are known.\n\nThroughout this lesson, we assume that samples from two groups, $X$ and $Y,$ are **independent.** Two groups are independent if they are not directly related or connected. Specifically:\n\n- The selection or outcome of one group does not influence the selection or outcome of the other.\n\n- The individuals or data points within each group are not paired or matched in any way, and there is no inherent relationship between the observations in the two groups.\n\nFor example, suppose an educational institution wants to test the effectiveness of a new exam format. The institution believes that students perform better under the new format. To assess this belief, they could conduct independent or dependent samples. Let's discuss an example of each.\n\n**Independent Samples**\n\nThe institution randomly selects 100 students, 50 of whom are randomly assigned to sit the exam under the new format (the experimental group), and 50 to take the exam under the traditional format (the control group). Both groups sit their respective exams, and the results are compared.\n\nIn this case, the experimental and control groups represent independent samples, as the outcomes in one group are not influenced by the outcomes in the other group, and the students are not paired or matched in any way.\n\n**Dependent Samples**\n\nThe institution randomly selects 50 students. First, each student sits the exam under the traditional format, and the results are recorded (baseline measurement). Then, all students take the exam under the new format, and their performance is measured again (post-experiment measurement).\n\nIn this case, the two groups (baseline and post-experiment measurements) represent dependent samples. This is because the data is collected from the same set of students, and each student's post-experiment performance is related to their baseline performance." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173251 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have two normally distributed populations $X$ and $Y,$ where\n\n$$\nX\\sim N(\\mu_x, \\sigma^2_x), \\qquad Y\\sim N(\\mu_y, \\sigma^2_y).\n$$\n\n \n\nIf we conduct a random sample of size $n_x$ from the first population and a second, *independent* random sample of size $n_y$ from the second population, then we know that\n\n$$\n\\overline{X}\\sim N\\left(\\mu_x,\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x}\\right), \\qquad \\overline{Y}\\sim N\\left(\\mu_y,\\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}\\right),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means. \n\nNow, we know that the sum or difference of two independent normally distributed random variables is also normal. Moreover, using our usual results for subtracting means and variances, we have\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left(\\mu_x - \\mu_y,\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y} \\right).\n$$\n\nThis means that the random variable $Z,$ defined as\n\n$$\nZ =\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a standard normal distribution $N(0,1).$\n\nLet's look at a concrete example that uses this result to construct a confidence interval." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173214 :tutorial/content "Suppose we draw two independent random samples of sizes $n_x=10$ and $n_y=15$ from two normally distributed populations $X$ and $Y$ with *unknown* population means $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ but *known* population variances $\\sigma_x^2=9$ and $\\sigma_y^2=10,$ respectively. \n\nAfter processing our results, we find that the means of these samples are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overline{x}=14, \\qquad\n\\overline{y}=11\n$$\n\n \n\nThese are unbiased point estimates of the corresponding population means $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y.$ \n\nNow, by computing the difference \n\n$$\n\\overline{x} - \\overline{y} = 14 - 11 = 3,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain a point estimate of the difference \n\n$$\n\\mu_x - \\mu_y.\n$$\n\nHowever, instead of reporting a single estimate $\\overline{x}-\\overline{y},$ it's often helpful to give a confidence interval of the form\n\n$$\n\\Big( (\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})-e, \\: (\\overline{x}-\\overline{y})+e \\Big),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $e$ is some margin of error.\n\nLet's now discuss how to compute a confidence interval in this case." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173215 :tutorial/content "In our example, the samples are independent, and the populations are normally distributed. Therefore, the difference of sample means is also normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y} \\sim N\\left( \\mu_x-\\mu_y,  \\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}\\right).\n$$\n\nBy transforming $\\overline{X}-\\overline{Y}$ to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n$$\nZ = \\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} \\sim N(0,1).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find a $z$-interval that we're $95\\%$ confident that the random variable $Z$ lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\n  \n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter $\\alpha.$ Since we're computing a $95\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha=1-0.95=0.05\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as $\\pm z_{\\alpha/2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n$$\nP(Z > z_{0.025}) = P(Z < -z_{0.025}) = 0.025.\n$$\n\n  \n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that $z_{0.025} \\approx 1.96.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z > 1.96) = P(Z < -1.96) = 0.025.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-1.96<Z<1.96)=0.95.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, there is a $95\\%$ probability that our random variable $Z$ will lie in the interval $(-1.96, 1.96).$\n\nLet's consider the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement: \n\n$$\n-1.96 < Z < 1.96\n$$\n\nWriting this in terms of our original variables, we have:\n\n$$\n-1.96 < \\:\\dfrac{\\overline{x} - \\overline{y} - (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}} < 1.96\n$$\n\nSolving this for the difference $\\mu_x-\\mu_y,$ we get \n\n$$\n(\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}) - 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}} < \\: \\mu_x - \\mu_y < (\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}) + 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}} .\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_x - \\mu_y$ of population means is given by\n\n$$\n\\left((\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}) - 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}},\\: (\\overline{x} - \\overline{y}) + 1.96 \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}} \\: \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n$$\nn_x=10, \\quad\nn_y=15, \\quad\n\\overline{x}=14, \\quad\n\\overline{y}=11, \\quad\n\\sigma_x^2=9, \\quad \n\\sigma_y^2=10,\n$$\n\nwe obtain that our $95\\%$ confidence interval for $\\mu_x-\\mu_y$ is \n\n$$\n\\big( 3 - 2.453, \\: 3 + 2.453\\big).\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's now discuss the general procedure." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173242 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have independent random samples of sizes $n_x$ and $n_y$ from normal populations with unknown population means $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ and *known* population variances $\\sigma_x^2$ and $\\sigma_y^2.$ \n\nFor a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_x - \\mu_y$ between the population means is given by\n\n$$\n\\left((\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}, \\: (\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}} \\: \\right)\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2},$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1).$ \n\nThe confidence limits (i.e., endpoints of our interval) are given by\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) \\: \\pm \\: z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}.\n$$\n\nAs usual, each part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}$ is an estimate of the difference of the population means\n\n- $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}$ is the standard error\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/67/6711538f5dd66f698fd39bc9e2f6e4e6be86e1a5b4d7392ec097822c67328466.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ ((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ ((\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{x}^{2}}{{n}_{x}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{y}^{2}}{{n}_{y}}}, & \\,(\\overset{―}{x}-\\overset{―}{y})+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{x}^{2}}{{n}_{x}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{y}^{2}}{{n}_{y}}}\\,)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173243 :tutorial/content "Up to now, we've assumed that the underlying populations are normally distributed. However, if both samples are *sufficiently large,* then according to the central limit theorem, we can use the approximation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X} - \\overline{Y}- (\\mu_x - \\mu_y)}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}}  \\approx N(0,1).\n$$\n\nTypically, \"sufficiently large\" means that $n_x \\geq 30$ *and* $n_y\\geq 30.$\n\nSo, even though we might not know the underlying distribution, we can still use the following formula for the confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n(\\overline{x}-\\overline{y}) \\: \\pm \\: z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sigma_x^2}{n_x} + \\dfrac{\\sigma_y^2}{n_y}}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186173216 :tutorial/content "A confidence interval for the difference $\\mu_x-\\mu_y$ between the population means can be interpreted in the following ways:\n\n- If all values within the confidence interval are *positive*, this suggests that $\\mu_x - \\mu_y > 0.$ In other words \n\n$$\n\\mu_x > \\mu_y.\n$$\n\nWe should carry out a statistical hypothesis test to formally verify this claim.\n\n- If all values within the confidence interval are *negative*, this suggests that $\\mu_x - \\mu_y < 0.$ In other words \n\n$$\n\\mu_x < \\mu_y.\n$$\n\nAgain, we should conduct a statistical hypothesis test to verify this claim.\n\n- If the confidence interval contains zero, then there is *insufficient evidence* of a statistically significant difference between the two means. Therefore, we conclude\n\n$$\n\\mu_x \\approx \\mu_y.\n$$\n\nLet's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172147 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176031 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a7eddd04-feb6-5c48-8a92-ed857f442343"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176032 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c4533ef5-ffb3-5002-a380-a8c4258911ab"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a7eddd04-feb6-5c48-8a92-ed857f442343" :question/id #uuid "a7eddd04-feb6-5c48-8a92-ed857f442343" :question/math-academy-id "q-134181" :question/problem "It takes $7$ hours for a snail to crawl $210$ centimeters, moving at a constant pace. Find the distance this snail covers in $4$ hours." :question/worked-solution "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed in centimeters per hour, we divide the number of centimeters $210,$ by the number of hours, $7.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{210}{7} & =210÷7 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the snail is $30$ centimeters per hour. \n\nTo find the distance covered in $4$ hours, we multiply the speed by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{distance} & =\\text{speed}\\times \\text{time} \\\\  & =30\\times 4 \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the snail covers $120$ centimeters in $4$ hours." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b26637dd-984f-503a-b1bf-c1c32324caf1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b26637dd-984f-503a-b1bf-c1c32324caf1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b26637dd-984f-503a-b1bf-c1c32324caf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e12ebe36-7b11-565b-978c-88a0da49eb43" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e12ebe36-7b11-565b-978c-88a0da49eb43" "answer-4353a9db-b473-521b-afcd-4f39cbe7360e" "answer-7ac2013f-3262-50b2-a5fb-d85c51fa13e8" "answer-a3403e5b-d04c-56d1-9ac1-d68c45e74f4d" "answer-f468dce8-5f7b-51d5-b613-15bb9d6144b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e12ebe36-7b11-565b-978c-88a0da49eb43" :answer/id #uuid "e12ebe36-7b11-565b-978c-88a0da49eb43" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$120$ centimeters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4353a9db-b473-521b-afcd-4f39cbe7360e" :answer/id #uuid "4353a9db-b473-521b-afcd-4f39cbe7360e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$160$ centimeters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ac2013f-3262-50b2-a5fb-d85c51fa13e8" :answer/id #uuid "7ac2013f-3262-50b2-a5fb-d85c51fa13e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$180$ centimeters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a3403e5b-d04c-56d1-9ac1-d68c45e74f4d" :answer/id #uuid "a3403e5b-d04c-56d1-9ac1-d68c45e74f4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$40$ centimeters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f468dce8-5f7b-51d5-b613-15bb9d6144b6" :answer/id #uuid "f468dce8-5f7b-51d5-b613-15bb9d6144b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$70$ centimeters" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c4533ef5-ffb3-5002-a380-a8c4258911ab" :question/id #uuid "c4533ef5-ffb3-5002-a380-a8c4258911ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-134223" :question/problem "An arrow takes $3$ seconds to travel $360$ meters at a constant pace. How long does it take for the arrow to travel $660$ meters?" :question/worked-solution "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in meters per second, we divide the number of meters, which is $360,$ by the number of seconds, $3.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{360}{3} & =360÷3 \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the arrow is $120$ meters per second. \n\nTo find the time it takes the arrow to cover $660$ meters, we divide the distance by $120.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{660}{120} \\\\  & =\\frac{660÷60}{120÷60} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =11÷2 \\\\  & =5.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the arrow $5.5$ seconds to travel $660$ meters." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-06e15d93-75fb-556b-b60c-527efedc085b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-06e15d93-75fb-556b-b60c-527efedc085b" :answer-field/id #uuid "06e15d93-75fb-556b-b60c-527efedc085b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d027ce92-f72a-5905-a777-3dcb97363e92" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ed8e9a5a-648c-5e35-a626-fdd51fb3f968" "answer-b8f61d81-282c-5df4-866e-98af9e656943" "answer-d027ce92-f72a-5905-a777-3dcb97363e92" "answer-b311435e-2bd0-5d50-a745-6762c75ecb40" "answer-89162f4f-cecd-50dc-8b82-62ddcbb54263"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed8e9a5a-648c-5e35-a626-fdd51fb3f968" :answer/id #uuid "ed8e9a5a-648c-5e35-a626-fdd51fb3f968" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$4.5$ seconds" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8f61d81-282c-5df4-866e-98af9e656943" :answer/id #uuid "b8f61d81-282c-5df4-866e-98af9e656943" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5$ seconds" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d027ce92-f72a-5905-a777-3dcb97363e92" :answer/id #uuid "d027ce92-f72a-5905-a777-3dcb97363e92" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$5.5$ seconds" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b311435e-2bd0-5d50-a745-6762c75ecb40" :answer/id #uuid "b311435e-2bd0-5d50-a745-6762c75ecb40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$6$ seconds" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89162f4f-cecd-50dc-8b82-62ddcbb54263" :answer/id #uuid "89162f4f-cecd-50dc-8b82-62ddcbb54263" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$6.5$ seconds" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176332 :question/worked-solution "Speed is a special unit rate applied to moving objects. It is defined as *the distance traveled per unit of time*:\n\n$$\n\\text{speed}=\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{time}}\n$$\n\nTo find the speed, in centimeters per minute, we divide the number of centimeters, which is $90,$ by the number of minutes, $20.$ This gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{90}{20} & =\\frac{90÷10}{20÷10} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =9÷2 \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the slug is $4.5$ centimeters per minute. \n\nTo find the time it took the slug to cover $450$ centimeters, we divide the distance by $4.5.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{time} & =\\frac{\\text{distance}}{\\text{speed}} \\\\  & =\\frac{450}{4.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{450\\cdot 10}{4.5\\cdot 10} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\,500}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{45\\cdot 100}{45} \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes the slug $100$ minutes to crawl $450$ centimeters." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186174250 :answer-field/correct 17592186174253 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176136 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186073136 :answer-field/choices 17592186073137] [:db/retract 17592186073136 :answer-field/choices 17592186073138] [:db/retract 17592186073136 :answer-field/choices 17592186073139] [:db/retract 17592186073136 :answer-field/choices 17592186073140] [:db/retract 17592186073136 :answer-field/choices 17592186073141] [:db/retract 17592186073070 :answer-field/choices 17592186073072] [:db/retract 17592186073070 :answer-field/choices 17592186073073] [:db/retract 17592186073070 :answer-field/choices 17592186073074] [:db/retract 17592186073070 :answer-field/choices 17592186073075] [:db/retract 17592186073070 :answer-field/choices 17592186073071] {:db/id 17592186073852 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0eac2215-e323-598c-aeac-4408994fc7bf" "question-c899bac6-ef39-565f-bca3-4cc3481908c4" "question-22cfb00e-d576-505c-8cf4-a3b624bb7d83"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073567 :question/worked-solution "The tape diagram for big tokens shows $4$ boxes. So, there are $4$ big tokens.\n\nAltogether, there are $12+4=16$ boxes. So, there are $16$ tokens in all.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of big tokens to all tokens is $4:16.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073853 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-24b62cd6-a2c3-5060-a0de-7e8afa941a63" "question-0da3c985-9149-5021-920d-90e4d419fc72" "question-15b8eebd-946d-5aa5-9960-ed6597145975" "question-cc35b4b8-7e0d-5fe0-bbed-0dea389c7b90" "question-f7a86a9c-206d-5e61-b545-baa0dd6589ec"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073569 :question/worked-solution "We are told that:\n\nAbbie makes $\\color{blue}5$ cupcakes for every $\\color{red}7$ that Kate makes.\n\nSo, in a tape diagram, Abbie's tape must contain $\\color{blue}5$ boxes, while Kate's tape should have $\\color{red}7$ boxes.\n\nTherefore, the diagram is the following:\n\n![](images/47/47c6e186cd17bffc7e83cca0c228bc1d05287cfba1bdb394c0a5dd71d411294a.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073854 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-53afd0b5-618f-512e-b769-550096e6ab34" "question-c2d3a89a-3b53-50ea-ab1b-27e8bcd50832" "question-e4c72bae-b132-52e1-8c37-a59f0f9c3c2e" "question-29d68311-d734-5664-956b-1dbe021c610a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073574 :question/worked-solution "We are told that there are $54$ orange plastic building blocks. The tape diagram for orange plastic building blocks shows $6$ boxes. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $54 \\div 6 = 9$ plastic building blocks.\n\nNow, we fill the number $9$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/16/16c543fb48a3d3c89c36b969cd4fceec36dd0c880664fd979c76740fd12c5722.png)\n\nSince there are $10$ boxes corresponding to the plastic building blocks in total, and each box represents $9$ plastic building blocks, the total number of plastic building blocks is:\n\n$$\n10 \\times 9 = 90\n$$\n\nSo, there are $90$ plastic building blocks." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073855 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-05816576-2c37-58bb-af9f-d158df6b7282" "question-ed7cfda9-6dae-5e39-9991-44b62b4e52f7" "question-73f7645f-5556-5602-99d1-a98b9d1aa7ab" "question-1a689f52-7a55-519b-a705-abe55d84d915"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073575 :question/worked-solution "The tape diagram shows $1$ box corresponding to winning tickets, and $4$ boxes corresponding to nonwinning tickets. Therefore, the ratio of winning to nonwinning tickets is $1:4.$\n\nWe are told that there are $60$ tickets in total. The tape diagram has $1+4=5$ boxes in total. Therefore, each box in the diagram above represents $60 \\div 5 = 12$ tickets.\n\nNow, we fill the number $12$ in each box of both tapes:\n\n![](images/16/1669ac0d1aa6edd5bf53609121080dc8e8b685b57daa60b91daf3db816474ef2.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ boxes corresponding to nonwinning tickets, and each box represents $12$ tickets, the number of nonwinning tickets must be:\n\n$$\n4 \\times 12 = 48.\n$$\n\nSo, there are $48$ nonwinning tickets in the jar." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186074483 :question/worked-solution "The tape diagram for cheap soaps shows $6$ boxes while the tape diagram has $6+3=9$ boxes in total.\n\nTherefore, the ratio of cheap soaps to all soaps is $6:9.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073136 :answer-field/correct "answer-a1c04286-ecf9-5d7c-8cb5-a29a06933ec0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db75d2ab-9304-5f56-